(window.GAMES = window.GAMES || {})[1993] = [{"t":"3 Count Bout","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Sports","r":"March 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3_Count_Bout","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4hhh.jpg","s":"3 Count Bout is a one-on-one fighting game. Choose from ten wrestlers who have their own power attacks. There are ten rounds in the game, and each one of them has you fighting other wrestlers that are much tougher than the previous ones. You defeat each one by biting, kicking, and performing other moves to the point where their damage meter is empty. But just because it is empty doesn't mean that you win. You have to pin him for the infamous-three count. Also features competitive play and tag match battles.\n\n3 Count Bout by SNK Corporation attempts to take you into the world of professional wrestling by giving you control of one of many different wrestlers as you fight both in the ring and in the seedy underworld in your quest to become an accomplished wrestling champion. Choose from one of ten different characters, each with his own strengths and weaknesses, and employ your wrestling moves using a combination of three buttons. There are also a number of hidden moves and combinations which can be unlocked for even greater variety in the gameplay. When you have your opponent on the mat, keep him there until the count of three and you've won the match. In two-player mode, you can either take on your opponent in a match or wrestle tag team. This game uses the Neo-Geo MVS cartridge system."},{"t":"3-D Dinosaur Adventure","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Knowledge Adventure","u":"Knowledge Adventure","g":"Educational","r":"December 20, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3-D_Dinosaur_Adventure"},{"t":"3rd Super Robot Wars","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Winkysoft","u":"Banpresto","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"July 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3rd_Super_Robot_Wars"},{"t":"A-Rank Thunder Tanjouhen","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Telenet Japan","u":"Riot","g":"Adventure","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A-Rank_Thunder_Tanjouhen"},{"t":"Aah! Harimanada","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aah!_Harimanada"},{"t":"Accele Brid","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Genki","u":"Tomy","g":"Action, Shooter","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accele_Brid"},{"t":"ActRaiser 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Quintet, Ancient","u":"Enix, Ubi Soft","g":"Platform","r":"October 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ActRaiser_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co287p.jpg","s":"ActRaiser 2 is a side-scrolling platform game for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System developed by Quintet and published by Enix in 1993.\n\nThe game is a sequel to the original ActRaiser, but the storyline is not directly connected to its predecessor; however, many plot details suggest that ActRaiser 2 may actually be set before the events of the original ActRaiser, or takes place in another universe entirely. Otherwise, the given story draws concepts from the famous religious epics Paradise Lost and the Divine Comedy.\n\nUnlike the original game, which alternately combined platform game sequences and god game sequences, ActRaiser 2 is only a platform game."},{"t":"Aero the Acro-Bat","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Iguana Entertainment","u":"Sunsoft, Metro 3D, Zoo Digital, Ratalaika Games","g":"Platform","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aero_the_Acro-Bat","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2h94.jpg","s":"You play as bat Aero, who have to stop the mad scientist Edgar Ektor, who is trying to rid the world of amusement and fun. So you jump through the circus-style levels, using different kinds of machines such as catapults, cannons, bubble machines, platforms, etc., collecting various power-ups such as cheese, soda, keys, clocks, etc. and avoiding lethal obstacles."},{"t":"Aerobiz Supersonic","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","PC-9800 series","Windows","MS-DOS"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Business simulation","r":"April 2, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerobiz_Supersonic"},{"t":"Aim for the Ace!","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aim_for_the_Ace!_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Albert Odyssey","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sunsoft","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Tactical role-playing game","r":"March 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Odyssey_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Alcahest","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"Square","g":"Action","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcahest_(video_game)"},{"t":"Alfred Chicken","p":["Game Boy","Amiga","Amiga CD32","Nintendo Entertainment System","PlayStation"],"d":"Twilight, Möbius Entertainment","u":"Mindscape, SCi Games, Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Chicken"},{"t":"Alien 3: The Gun","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Rail shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_3:_The_Gun","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5fns.jpg","s":"Using cabinet mounted guns you and another player blast away the Aliens in an attempt to save the prison planet. You are also given a limited number of grenades to use when the going gets tough. The gameplay is very close to Sega’s other lightgun games from the period such as Gunblade and Jurassic Park. This game was based on the movie of the same name."},{"t":"Alien Breed II: The Horror Continues","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"Team17","u":"Team17","g":"Run and gun","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Breed_II:_The_Horror_Continues"},{"t":"Alien Carnage","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Interactive Binary Illusions/SubZero Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Side-scrolling platform game","r":"October 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Carnage"},{"t":"Alien Gate","p":["CD-i"],"d":"SPC Vision","u":"Philips","g":"Fixed shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Gate"},{"t":"Alien vs Predator","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Jorudan","u":"IGS, Activision","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"January 8, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_vs_Predator_(SNES_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9fll.jpg","s":"Based on the Alien versus Predator comic book series. During the 25th century, mankind has colonized many planets. On one of these, New Shanghai, strange creatures have been discovered. These aliens cause much havoc. New Shanghai initiates a distress call to other planets in the galaxy. A cloaked ship belonging to hunting creatures known as predators intercepts the message. The predators on board decide that these strange alien creatures might make worthy trophies for the collection. The player takes the role of one of the predators."},{"t":"Alien vs Predator: The Last of His Clan","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_vs_Predator:_The_Last_of_His_Clan"},{"t":"Alone in the Dark 2","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Infogrames, Arrow Micro-Techs Corp, Krisalis, Brainstorm Software","u":"Infogrames, Kokopeli Digital Studios, Interplay Productions, Infogrames Multimedia","g":"Survival horror","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alone_in_the_Dark_2_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co82bf.jpg","s":"Alone in the Dark 2 is the second instalment in the series and a direct sequel to Alone in the Dark. Like its predecessor, the game uses hybrid 2D–3D graphics and requires players to solve numerous puzzles while fighting zombie pirates along the way. Some enemies can be killed using the wide range of weapons at the player’s disposal, while others must be defeated through trickery.\n\nIn addition to Edward Carnby, the game also includes a short section in which players control young Grace Saunders. Grace cannot fight, and the sections featuring her focus instead on quick reflexes and wits to evade and outsmart enemies."},{"t":"Ambermoon","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Thalion Software","u":"Thalion Software","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambermoon"},{"t":"Ambush at Sorinor","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Mindcraft","u":"Mindcraft","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambush_at_Sorinor"},{"t":"Andre Agassi Tennis","p":["Sega Master System","Game Gear","Genesis / Mega Drive","Mobile phone"],"d":"TecMagik","u":"TecMagik","g":"Sports","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andre_Agassi_Tennis"},{"t":"Android Assault: The Revenge of Bari-Arm","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment, Big Fun Games","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"July 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Android_Assault:_The_Revenge_of_Bari-Arm"},{"t":"Anett Futatabi","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Wolf Team","u":"Wolf Team","g":"Beat 'em up, hack and slash","r":"March 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anett_Futatabi"},{"t":"APBA presents: Baseball for Windows","p":["Windows"],"d":"Miller Associates","u":"Miller Associates","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/APBA_presents:_Baseball_for_Windows"},{"t":"Arabian Nights","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"Krisalis Software","u":"Krisalis Software","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabian_Nights_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Ardy Lightfoot","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"ASCII Corporation","u":"ASCII Corporation, Titus Interactive","g":"Platform","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardy_Lightfoot","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2l21.jpg","s":"The story of the game is that the sacred rainbow has shattered into seven pieces and it's up to Ardy to obtain them all again. Whoever collects all seven pieces will receive one wish. An evil king named Visconti has already gotten one piece and is searching for the others. To this end he sends out various creatures and henchmen such as Beecroft, Catry and others. These creatures form the opponents for Ardy during the game. Ardy is assisted by friends along the way like the elder (unknown name), Nina, and a mysterious adventurer named Don Jacoby."},{"t":"Arena Football","p":["Commodore 64"],"d":"Chris R. Bickford III","u":"C159 Production","g":"American football","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arena_Football_(1988_video_game)"},{"t":"Arnie 2","p":["Amiga","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Zeppelin Games","u":"Zeppelin Games","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnie_2"},{"t":"Asterix","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Bit Managers, Infogrames","u":"Infogrames","g":"2D action platformer","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asterix_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9jci.jpg","s":"Astérix was developed by Bit Managers and Infogrames for a number of different consoles. It was based after the comic book series Asterix, which maintained a high level of popularity in Europe, but never took off in North America. For this reason, the game was only released in PAL format, and was never released in North America."},{"t":"Asterix and the Great Rescue","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Core Design","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asterix_and_the_Great_Rescue","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4ebr.jpg","s":"It is 50 B.C., and the entire Gaul (today's France) is conquered by the Romans. Only one village still remains independent, thanks to the incredible powers of its two great warriors, Asterix and Obelix. In the game, they must first of all liberate the village Orvio and rescue their friend Dogmatix.\n\nThe player can play as either Asterix or Obelix. They make their way through platform levels, fighting enemies by punching them, and jumping to access higher ares or to avoid falling down. There are also some items that the player can collect, such as bombs, with which the player can overcome obstacles and advance in the game."},{"t":"Atlantis PbeM","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis_PbeM"},{"t":"Awesome Possum... Kicks Dr. Machino's Butt","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Tengen","u":"Tengen","g":"Platform","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Awesome_Possum..._Kicks_Dr._Machino's_Butt"},{"t":"B.O.B.","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Gray Matter, Foley Hi-Tech Systems","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Run and gun","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B.O.B._(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1nvo.jpg","s":"B.O.B. (known in Japan as Space Funky B.O.B.) is a video game that was released in 1993. It is a side-scrolling game, a 2D shooter and a platform game."},{"t":"Baby T-Rex","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Laser Beam Entertainment, Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Platform","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baby_T-Rex"},{"t":"Ball Jacks","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Action","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ball_Jacks"},{"t":"Barbie: Super Model","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Tahoe Software Productions","u":"Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Educational, action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbie:_Super_Model"},{"t":"Barney's Hide & Seek Game","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Realtime Associates","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barney's_Hide_&_Seek_Game"},{"t":"Batman Returns","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman_Returns_(NES_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3bsp.jpg","s":"The SNES version of the game was released in 1993. It is fundamentally a left-to-right scrolling fighter beat 'em up, a genre that was featured heavily on the console at the time. The gameplay and graphics are very similar to the Final Fight games. The game takes the player through seven scenes featured in the film. Each scene has a boss fight that Batman must win in order to proceed to the next scene."},{"t":"Batman Returns","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"February 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman_Returns_(SNES_video_game)"},{"t":"Batman: The Animated Series","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platformer","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman:_The_Animated_Series_(video_game)"},{"t":"Batsugun","p":["Arcade","Sega Saturn","Windows","macOS","Linux"],"d":"Toaplan, exA-Arcadia","u":"Toaplan, Taito, Unite Trading, exA-Arcadia","g":"Scrolling shooter, bullet hell","r":"December 7, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batsugun","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cocse0.jpg","s":"Batsugun is a vertically scrolling shoot 'em up video game created by the now-defunct Japanese game developer Toaplan. The title translates to \"exceptional\" or \"extraordinary\".\n\nIt was the final game created before Toaplan's dissolution, and has been regarded as a key title in the development of the modern manic shooter, with elements of the game such as the size of the player's hitbox, the scale of their firepower, and the complexity of enemy bullet patterns all having an influence on future titles in the sub-genre. Members of the development team also went on to work on other successful manic shooters, most notably lead artist Junya Inoue."},{"t":"Battle Cars","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Malibu Interactive","u":"Namco Hometek","g":"Racing, Vehicular combat","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Cars"},{"t":"Battle Lode Runner","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"February 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Lode_Runner"},{"t":"Battle Mania Daiginjou","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Vic Tokai","g":"Shooter","r":"December 24, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/battle-mania-daiginjou","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4ahf.jpg","s":"Battle Mania Daiginjou is a shoot 'em up developed and published by VIC Tokai in 1993 for the Sega Mega Drive exclusively in Japan and South Korea. It is the sequel to Battle Mania (which was released in the US as Trouble Shooter)."},{"t":"Battle Mania Daiginjō","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Vic Tokai","u":"Vic Tokai","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"December 24, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Mania_Daiginj%C5%8D"},{"t":"Battle Rush: Build Up Robot Tournament","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"TOSE","u":"B-AI","g":"Action","r":"November 13, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Rush:_Build_Up_Robot_Tournament"},{"t":"Battleship","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Gear"],"d":"Mindscape UK, NGM Productions","u":"Mindscape","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battleship_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Battletoads in Battlemaniacs","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Rare","u":"Tradewest, Nintendo, Masaya, Tec Toy","g":"Beat 'em up, platform","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battletoads_in_Battlemaniacs","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2h93.jpg","s":"Battletoads in Battlemaniacs is a platforming beat 'em up. The game follows two Battletoads, Rash and Pimple, on a quest to stop Silas Volkmire and the evil Dark Queen from ruling over the world. Many of its levels are enhanced or remixed versions of levels from the original Battletoads, featuring similar mechanics and gameplay styles."},{"t":"Battletoads/Double Dragon","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Rare","u":"Tradewest, Nintendo, Sony Imagesoft","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battletoads/Double_Dragon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co70dr.jpg","s":"Battletoads & Double Dragon - The Ultimate Team is the fourth game in the Battletoads series. It is a crossover with the Double Dragon series of beat 'em up games developed by Technos Japan. It was released for the NES, with ports for the Super NES, Sega Mega Drive/Genesis, and Nintendo Game Boy. Despite the presence of the Double Dragon characters, Technos Japan had no actual role in the game's development."},{"t":"BattleWheels","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Beyond Games","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"First-person shooter, vehicular combat","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BattleWheels"},{"t":"Beethoven: The Ultimate Canine Caper","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","MS-DOS"],"d":"Riedel Software Productions, Rozner Labs Software","u":"Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Platform","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beethoven:_The_Ultimate_Canine_Caper"},{"t":"Betrayal at Krondor","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Role-playing","r":"June 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betrayal_at_Krondor","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co26ek.jpg","s":"Betrayal at Krondor: Midkemia – a realm of enchantment and ancient magic where elves, dwarves and man once battled an unimaginable evil across rifts in time and space. The legends tell of a boy named Pug who would become Master Magician. His friend, Tomas, would wield the unearthly armor of the Valheru and command the armies of Elvandar to fight the final titanic battle at Sethanon. In Betrayal at Krondor experience an all-new tale in the Riftwar legacy as you travel the world of Ramar."},{"t":"Beyond Shadowgate","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"ICOM Simulations, Inc.","u":"Turbo Technologies Inc.","g":"Adventure","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyond_Shadowgate_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Bill Walsh College Football","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega CD"],"d":"High Score Productions, Visual Concepts","u":"EA Sports","g":"Sports, American football","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Walsh_College_Football"},{"t":"Bio Menace","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS","Linux"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform","r":"August 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bio_Menace","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co507b.jpg","s":"Intelligence reports indicate that a man calling himself Dr. Mangle is responsible for the mutants and the destruction of Metro. You will have to battle your way through levels packed with evil mutants and robotic guards to finally infiltrate Mangle's fortress and defeat him. You think to yourself that this will be the most dangerous mission of your life.\n\nSnake Logan is a weapons expert, and uses several throughout the game, including a high-powered machine gun and grenades."},{"t":"BioMetal","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Athena","u":"Athena, Activision","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BioMetal_(video_game)"},{"t":"Bishōjo Senshi Sailor Moon R","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Angel","u":"Bandai","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"December 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bish%C5%8Djo_Senshi_Sailor_Moon_R_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Blades of Vengeance","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Platform","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blades_of_Vengeance","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5k26.jpg","s":"Blades of Vengeance is a platform game that was developed by Beam Software and published by Electronic Arts for the Sega Mega Drive/Genesis. In this game, one or two players can select one of three fantasy warriors to fight through a large range of platform levels in order to defeat the forces of darkness."},{"t":"Blake Stone: Aliens of Gold","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS","Linux","Amiga"],"d":"JAM Productions","u":"Apogee Software","g":"First-person shooter","r":"December 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blake_Stone:_Aliens_of_Gold","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2hlx.jpg","s":"You're pitted against Dr. Pyrus Goldfire. He's found a way to replicate pure gold, which he's using to fund his maniacal plan. Goldfire has built six highly-secure, futuristic locations where his creations are being hatched. It's up to you to penetrate his security and stop him at all costs."},{"t":"Blaster Master 2","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Run and gun","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaster_Master_2"},{"t":"Blob","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Jonnathan Hilliard","u":"Core Design","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"September 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blob_(video_game)"},{"t":"BloodNet","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"MicroProse Greensburg","u":"MicroProse","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BloodNet"},{"t":"Bloodstone: An Epic Dwarven Tale","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Mindcraft Software, Inc.","u":"Mindcraft Software, Inc.","g":"Role-playing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloodstone:_An_Epic_Dwarven_Tale"},{"t":"Blue Force","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Tsunami Games","u":"Tsunami Games","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Force"},{"t":"Body Blows","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Team17","u":"Team17","g":"Fighting","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_Blows"},{"t":"Body Blows Galactic","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Team17","u":"Team17","g":"Fighting game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_Blows_Galactic"},{"t":"Bomb Jack Twin","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"NMK","g":"Platform","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomb_Jack_Twin"},{"t":"Bomberman '94","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Hudson Soft, Westone","u":"Hudson Soft, Sega","g":"Action, maze","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomberman_'94","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9sg7.jpg","s":"BOMBERMAN has arrived to restore peace on the planet, which has been split into five parts by an evil hand! In addition to the nine members of the Bomber Family, the character ROOI shows up to lend a hand. Jump on the EGGS that appear from destroyed SOFT BLOCKS, and you can ride one of five types of ROOI. Each ROOI has different special abilitiesuse these well and youll ride to victory! Bombs that turn according to arrows and other new gimmicks have been added to BATTLE GAME, increased to ten stages. Tag-team matches are now possible, ensuring that this game will be a blast!\n\nBomberman '94 is a video game from the Bomberman series which was developed and published by Hudson Soft for the PC Engine and released on December 10, 1993 in Japan. It was later re-developed by Westone and re-published by Sega as Mega Bomberman on the Sega Mega Drive/Genesis in 1994 in other areas. The PC Engine Bomberman '94 was later released outside Japan through the Wii's Virtual Console and the PlayStation 3's PlayStation Network.\n\nThe game supports single-player and multi-player modes. In single player, the player navigates several levels of mazes, destroying creatures with bombs. In multi-player mode, players defeat each other with bombs.\n\nBomberman '94 is the final HuCard title to be made and released by Hudson Soft, the original designers of the PC Engine console."},{"t":"Bonk 3: Bonk's Big Adventure","p":["PC Engine","PC Engine CD"],"d":"A.I Co., Ltd.","u":"Hudson Soft, Turbo Technologies","g":"Platform","r":"April 2, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonk_3:_Bonk's_Big_Adventure"},{"t":"Boxing Legends of the Ring","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Electro Brain, Pack-In-Video","g":"Fighting, sports","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxing_Legends_of_the_Ring"},{"t":"Bram Stoker's Dracula","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Probe Entertainment","u":"Sony Imagesoft","g":"Action platformer","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bram_Stoker's_Dracula_(handheld_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6vqa.jpg","s":"Bram Stoker's Dracula is game loosely based on the 1992 film of the same name. It was released simultaneously for multiple consoles and while the plot remains largely the same, there are differing styles of gameplay and graphics across platforms.\n\nThis version of Bram Stoker's Dracula is a side-scrolling platforming action game where you play as the young lawyer named Jonathan Harker. Harker must free himself from Count Dracula's captivity, follow him to London, and end his reign of terror.\n\nThroughout the levels, Abraham Van Helsing will help Jonathan in his quest by providing advanced weapons. The game includes six different stages (each having between a number of areas, except for the final stage which only has one area) and fights various bosses, such as Lucy Westenra as a vampiress, Count Dracula's three brides, Dracula's coach driver, Dracula's fire-breathing dragon, Renfield, and even Dracula himself in multiple forms, such as his bat form, his young form, his evil wolf form and finally his knight form."},{"t":"Bram Stoker's Dracula","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear","Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Sega Master System","Sega CD"],"d":"Psygnosis, Traveller's Tales (Genesis and SNES, Probe Software","u":"Sony Imagesoft, Psygnosis","g":"Platform, Beat 'em up, First-person shooter","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bram_Stoker's_Dracula_(video_game)"},{"t":"Break Time: The National Pool Tour","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"u":"Fujisankei","g":"Sports","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Break_Time:_The_National_Pool_Tour"},{"t":"Breath of Fire","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Square Soft, Ubi Soft","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breath_of_Fire_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7up9.jpg","s":"Recognized by Capcom as their first traditional role-playing video game, Breath of Fire would set the precedent for future entries in the series, and features character designs by company head of development Keiji Inafune, as well as music by members of Capcom's in-house sound team Alph Lyla. In 2001, the game was re-released for the Game Boy Advance handheld system with new save features and minor graphical enhancements, with the English version being released in Europe for the first time.\n\nSet in a fantasy world, Breath of Fire follows the journey of a boy named Ryu, one of the last surviving members of an ancient race with the ability to transform into mighty dragons, as he searches the world for his sister. During his quest, Ryu meets other warriors who share his quest, and comes into conflict with the Dark Dragon Clan, a militaristic empire who seeks to take over the world by reviving a mad goddess. The game experienced mostly positive reception upon release, and was followed by a direct sequel, Breath of Fire II, in 1994."},{"t":"Brutal Sports Football","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Teque London","u":"Millennium Interactive, Telegames","g":"Sports","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brutal_Sports_Football"},{"t":"Bubble Bobble Part 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"ITL Co., Ltd., OLM","u":"Taito","g":"Platform","r":"March 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubble_Bobble_Part_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2375.jpg","s":"A new breed of heroes! Cubby and Rubby, descendants of the famous Bubby, must battle the Skull Brothers and their army of fiends to rescue a friend in danger. Fortunately, our dinosaur heroes can blow bubbles that will destroy their foes. They will rain fire, floods and tornado!"},{"t":"Bubsy in Claws Encounters of the Furred Kind","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Accolade, Solid Software, Al Baker & Associates, ATI Technologies","u":"Accolade, Pack-In-Video, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubsy_in_Claws_Encounters_of_the_Furred_Kind","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2sls.jpg","s":"Bubsy in Claws Encounters of the Furred Kind, known as Bubsy for short, is a platform video game released by Accolade in the early 1990s. It is the first game in the Bubsy series of video games. The game's name is a play on words in reference to Close Encounters of the Third Kind, with the game revolving around Bubsy defending the planet's supply of yarnballs from alien invaders. The game received a sequel, Bubsy 2, in 1994."},{"t":"Burning Rival","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega AM2","u":"Sega","g":"Fighting","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burning_Rival"},{"t":"Burntime","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Max Design","u":"Max Design","g":"Role-playing, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burntime"},{"t":"Buzz Aldrin's Race Into Space","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Strategic Visions","u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Turn-based strategy, Construction and management simulation","r":"March 11, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzz_Aldrin's_Race_Into_Space"},{"t":"BZFlag","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BZFlag"},{"t":"Cadillacs and Dinosaurs","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Side-scrolling, Beat 'em up","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadillacs_and_Dinosaurs_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2gh4.jpg","s":"Cadillacs and Dinosaurs is a side-scrolling, beat 'em up arcade game based on the comic book series Xenozoic Tales. In the game, the player has access to several attacks. Each character has two special moves including one that depletes a character's health upon contact with an enemy and when two or more players play together, they can trigger a team-attack. Players can also find and use various firearms, throwing weapons such as rocks and explosives, and melee weapons such as clubs."},{"t":"Call of Cthulhu: Shadow of the Comet","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Infogrames Europe","u":"Infogrames, Electronic Arts Victor, Imotion, Atari, Inc.","g":"Point-and-click, Puzzle, Adventure","r":"March 25, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/call-of-cthulhu-shadow-of-the-comet","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3wht.jpg","s":"You'll take on the role of John T. Parker, a brilliant young astronomer, piecing together the puzzling history of the village and its cryptic inhabitants. Incredibly realistic action and supernatural chaos intertwine to draw players deep into the puzzling mysteries within the Shadow of the Comet."},{"t":"Campaign II","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Empire Interactive","u":"Empire Interactive","g":"Wargame, Vehicle simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campaign_II"},{"t":"Cannon Fodder","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","Acorn Archimedes","MS-DOS"],"d":"Sensible Software","u":"Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"December 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannon_Fodder_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3o3f.jpg","s":"Cannon Fodder is an action-strategy shoot 'em up game developed by Sensible Software and published by Virgin Interactive. The game is military-themed and based on shooting action but with a strategy game-style control system. The player directs troops through numerous missions, battling enemy infantry, vehicles and installations.\n\nCannon Fodder has a darkly humorous tone which commentators variously praised and condemned. Its creators intended it to convey an anti-war message, which some reviewers recognised, but the Daily Star and a number of public figures derided the game. In other respects, reviewers highly praised the game, which widely achieved scores of over 90% in Amiga magazines. Amiga Action awarded it an unprecedented score, calling it the best game of the year."},{"t":"Capcom's MVP Football","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Equilibrium","u":"Capcom U.S.A.","g":"Sports","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capcom's_MVP_Football"},{"t":"Capitol Hill","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Amazing Media","u":"The Software Toolworks","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitol_Hill_(video_game)"},{"t":"Captain Tsubasa 4: Pro no Rival Tachi","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Traditional soccer simulation","r":"April 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captain_Tsubasa_4:_Pro_no_Rival_Tachi"},{"t":"Carriers at War II","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Strategic Studies Group","u":"Strategic Studies Group","g":"Wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carriers_at_War_II"},{"t":"Casino Kid","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"SOFEL","u":"SOFEL","g":"Casino","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casino_Kid"},{"t":"Castlevania Chronicles","p":["Sharp X68000"],"d":"Konami, KCET","u":"Konami","g":"Platform","r":"July 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castlevania_Chronicles"},{"t":"Castlevania: Rondo of Blood","p":["PC Engine","PC Engine CD"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platform","r":"October 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castlevania:_Rondo_of_Blood","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co20dk.jpg","s":"In Castlevania X: Rondo of Blood, the player takes control of Richter, the ultimate Vampire Killer, guiding him through a total of nine stages filled with treacherous traps and hidden secrets. Using your legendary whip – passed on from generations of Belmonts – and a powerful selection of Item Crash attacks, it's up to you to destroy Dracula and his evil horde. What's more, rescuing Maria at some juncture in the game allows you to use her as a playable character for even more vampire-slaying fun."},{"t":"Championship Manager 93/94","p":["Windows","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Sports Interactive","u":"Domark","g":"Sports","r":"September 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Championship_Manager_93/94"},{"t":"Championship Pool","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Genesis / Mega Drive","MS-DOS"],"d":"Bitmasters","u":"Mindscape","g":"Sports simulation – pool","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Championship_Pool"},{"t":"Chessmaster 4000 Turbo","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"The Software Toolworks","u":"The Software Toolworks, Mindscape","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chessmaster_4000_Turbo"},{"t":"Chester Cheetah","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chester_Cheetah"},{"t":"Chester Cheetah: Wild Wild Quest","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chester_Cheetah:_Wild_Wild_Quest"},{"t":"Chi Chi's Pro Challenge Golf","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Soft Vision","u":"Soft Vision, Virgin Games","g":"Traditional golf simulation","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chi_Chi's_Pro_Challenge_Golf"},{"t":"Chip 'n Dale Rescue Rangers 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Platform","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chip_'n_Dale_Rescue_Rangers_2"},{"t":"Choplifter III","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Teeny Weeny Games, Beam Software, Broderbund","u":"Ocean, Extreme Entertainment Group, Victor Interactive Software","g":"Shoot 'em up, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choplifter_III"},{"t":"Chuck Rock II: Son of Chuck","p":["Amiga","Game Gear","Sega CD","Sega Master System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Core Design","u":"Core Design, Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Rock_II:_Son_of_Chuck","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1p6n.jpg","s":"Chuck Rock II is a side-scrolling action game featuring the son of the protagonist Chuck Rock as he fights off dinosaurs and tries to help save his parents."},{"t":"Clash of Steel","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clash_of_Steel"},{"t":"ClayFighter","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Visual Concepts, Ringler Studios","u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Fighting","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ClayFighter_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2mnf.jpg","s":"Sticks and stones might make him groan, but clay really ticks him off! Bad Mister Frosty is the name, and action is the game. Fists and feet will fly (and other parts) as Frosty and his friends smack it out in head-to-head fighting action.\n\nClayFighter is a fighting game released for the Super NES in November 1993 and later ported to Sega Genesis in June 1994. The game is a parody of Street Fighter. In 2009, the Genesis version was re-released on the Wii Virtual Console."},{"t":"Claymates","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Visual Concepts","u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Platform","r":"November 20, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claymates"},{"t":"Cliffhanger","p":["Amiga","Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega CD","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Game Gear"],"d":"Spidersoft, Malibu Interactive","u":"Sony Imagesoft, Psygnosis Virgin Games, Infogrames","g":"Platform game, beat 'em up","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliffhanger_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2pre.jpg","s":"Standard side-scrolling fighter based off of the Stallone film of the same name. Sly must fight thugs and the elements as he tracks down suitcases of stolen cash dropped around the peaks of the Rockies. Combat follows the Final Fight standard, broken up by some mountain climbing and running (or 3-D snowboarding on the Sega CD) from avalanches.\n\nEnemies are the standard brawler crooks, occasionally carrying guns and knives the player can pick up. The game tends to follow a pattern of having you deal with a natural hazard (scaling a cliff, leaping over gaps), then locking the screen down for a fighting segment, nature again, fighting again, etc. The available fighting moves is limited, compared to other games of this type, and the ability to hit an enemy character is noticeably less accurate.\n\nThe plot is identical to the film, with levels based off of, or inspired by, scenes in the movie. Animated cutscenes move the story along on the cartridge systems, while brief clips from the film play between levels on the Sega CD."},{"t":"Coaster","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Code To Go","u":"Walt Disney Computer Software","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coaster_(video_game)"},{"t":"Color a Dinosaur","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"FarSight Studios","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Drawing and creativity","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_a_Dinosaur"},{"t":"Columns III","p":["Arcade","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega, Minato Giken","u":"Sega, Vic Tokai","g":"Puzzle","r":"October 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columns_III"},{"t":"Combat Air Patrol","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Psygnosis","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combat_Air_Patrol_(video_game)"},{"t":"Companions of Xanth","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Legend Entertainment","u":"Legend Entertainment","g":"Adventure","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Companions_of_Xanth"},{"t":"Cool Spot","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Sega Master System","Amiga"],"d":"Virgin Games","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Platform","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_Spot","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7rui.jpg","s":"Cool Spot is a mascot for 7 Up, introduced in 1987. During this time, the red spot in the 7 Up logo was anthropomorphized: given arms, legs, a mouth, and sunglasses. British video games developer Virgin Interactive's American studio produced a platform game starring the 7 Up Spot, entitled Cool Spot."},{"t":"Cool World","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Ocean","u":"Ocean","g":"Action","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_World_(NES_video_game)"},{"t":"Cosmic Spacehead","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Codemasters","u":"Codemasters","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/cosmic-spacehead","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3klf.jpg","s":"Cosmic needs your help! He has no money, no car, and no respect from his fellow Cosmonaut's! The trouble is, no one believed his tale of discovering a far away planet called \"Earth\" so it's your job to help him get back there and take some handy snaps! Starting from his home town Old Lino Town, it's Cosmic gameplay all the way in a unique blend of adventure and arcade action plus a whole galaxy of surprises thrown in!"},{"t":"Cosmology of Kyoto","p":["Windows","MS-DOS"],"d":"Softedge","u":"Yano Electric","g":"Adventure game, Horror, Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmology_of_Kyoto"},{"t":"Crash 'N Burn","p":["3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"Crystal Dynamics","u":"Crystal Dynamics","g":"Racing, vehicular combat","r":"October 4, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crash_'N_Burn_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Crayon Shin-Chan: Ora to Poi Poi","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"TOSE","u":"Bandai","g":"Action, Puzzle","r":"August 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crayon_Shin-Chan:_Ora_to_Poi_Poi"},{"t":"Creepers","p":[],"d":"Destiny Software Productions","u":"Psygnosis","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creepers_(video_game)"},{"t":"Crime Patrol","p":["Arcade","3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"American Laser Games, CapDisc, Digital Leisure","u":"American Laser Games, Atari Games, Namco, Philips Media","g":"Interactive movie, light gun shooter","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crime_Patrol_(video_game)"},{"t":"Crime Patrol 2: Drug Wars","p":["Arcade","3DO Interactive Multiplayer","CD-i"],"u":"American Laser Games, Digital Leisure","g":"Light gun shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crime_Patrol_2:_Drug_Wars"},{"t":"Crystal Caliburn","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"LittleWing","u":"StarPlay","g":"Pinball","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_Caliburn"},{"t":"Crystal Crazy","p":["macOS"],"u":"Casady & Greene","g":"Action game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_Crazy"},{"t":"Curses","p":[],"d":"Graham Nelson","u":"Self published","g":"Interactive Fiction, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curses_(video_game)"},{"t":"Cybattler","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Multidirectional shooter","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybattler"},{"t":"Cyber Chess","p":["Acorn Archimedes"],"d":"William Tunstall-Pedoe","u":"The Fourth Dimension","g":"Chess","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Chess"},{"t":"Cyber Sled","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco, Namco Hometek, Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Vehicular combat, shooter","r":"March 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Sled"},{"t":"Cybermorph","p":["Atari Jaguar"],"d":"Attention to Detail","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Shooter","r":"November 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybermorph"},{"t":"Cyberpunks","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Mutation","u":"Core Design","g":"Shoot 'em up - multi-scrolling","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyberpunks_(video_game)"},{"t":"CyberRace","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Cyberdreams","u":"Cyberdreams","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CyberRace"},{"t":"CyberStrike","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Simutronics","u":"MicroProse","g":"Mech simulation","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CyberStrike"},{"t":"Cyborg 009","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyborg_009_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Daffy Duck: The Marvin Missions","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"ICOM Simulations, Inc., Sunsoft","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Platform, action","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daffy_Duck:_The_Marvin_Missions","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3w88.jpg","s":"In this game, Duck Dodgers sets out to stop Marvin the Martian from performing various notorious deeds, including destroying his beloved home-planet, Earth. There seems to be only one way to stop Marvin - and that's to destroy all five of his deadly machines!"},{"t":"Daioh","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Athena","u":"Sammy Corporation","g":"Scrolling shooter, shoot 'em up","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daioh"},{"t":"Dangerous Streets","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","MS-DOS"],"d":"Micromania","u":"Flair Software","g":"Fighting","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dangerous_Streets"},{"t":"Darius Force","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Horizontal scrolling shooter","r":"September 24, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darius_Force"},{"t":"Dark Sun: Shattered Lands","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Sun:_Shattered_Lands","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8l4g.jpg","s":"Shattered Lands is the first in a series of computer role-playing games based in the highly acclaimed AD&D 2nd Edition DARK SUN game world. You begin the game as a gladiator about to fight in King Tectuktitlay’s Arena in Draj. You must survive that fight, along with many others, and continue onward to escape from the Slave Pens."},{"t":"Dark Wizard","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"November 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Wizard"},{"t":"Dashin' Desperadoes","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East","g":"Platform","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dashin'_Desperadoes"},{"t":"Day of the Tentacle","p":["macOS","MS-DOS"],"d":"LucasArts","u":"LucasArts","g":"Graphic adventure","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Day_of_the_Tentacle","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co30yt.jpg","s":"Day of the Tentacle, also known as Maniac Mansion II: Day of the Tentacle, is a 1993 graphic adventure game developed and published by LucasArts. It is the sequel to the 1987 game Maniac Mansion. The game's plot follows Bernard Bernoulli and his friends Hoagie and Laverne as they attempt to stop the evil Purple Tentacle — a sentient, disembodied tentacle — from taking over the world. The player takes control of the three and solves puzzles while using time travel to explore different periods of history.\n\nDave Grossman and Tim Schafer co-led the game's development, their first time in such a role. The pair carried over a limited amount of elements from Maniac Mansion and forwent the character selection aspect to simplify development. Inspirations included Chuck Jones cartoons and the history of the United States. Day of the Tentacle is the eighth LucasArts title to use the SCUMM engine, and the company's first title to feature voice acting.\n\nThe game was released simultaneously on floppy disk and CD-ROM to critical acclaim and commercial success. Critics focused on its cartoon-style visuals and comedic elements. Day of the Tentacle has featured regularly in lists of \"top\" games published more than two decades after its release, and aspects have been referenced in popular culture."},{"t":"Deep Core","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"Dynafield Systems","u":"International Computer Entertainment","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Core_(video_game)"},{"t":"Deep Duck Trouble Starring Donald Duck","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Aspect","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Duck_Trouble_Starring_Donald_Duck","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2b8d.jpg","s":"Uncle Scrooge found a magical necklace on a mysterious island, but the necklace put a curse on him. Now Uncle Scrooge is doomed to float around in the air forever! (Of course Donald always said Scrooge was full of hot air anyway!) To take the curse off Uncle Scrooge, Donald has to take the necklace back to the island. But there's plenty of danger awaiting Donald there. How about lending him a hand?"},{"t":"Dennis the Menace","p":["Amiga","Game Boy","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Ocean Software, Citizen Software","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Platform","r":"November 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennis_the_Menace_(video_game)"},{"t":"Desert Speedtrap","p":["Sega Master System","Game Gear"],"d":"Probe Software","u":"Sega","g":"Action","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desert_Speedtrap"},{"t":"Diggers","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","Acorn Archimedes","MS-DOS"],"d":"Millennium Interactive","u":"Millennium Interactive","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diggers_(video_game)"},{"t":"Dino Dini's Goal","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Dino Dini, Eurocom","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dino_Dini's_Goal"},{"t":"DinoPark Tycoon","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Manley & Associates","u":"MECC","g":"Business simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DinoPark_Tycoon"},{"t":"Dinosaurs for Hire","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega Interactive Development Division","u":"Sega","g":"Platform, shoot 'em up","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dinosaurs_for_Hire_(video_game)"},{"t":"Discoveries of the Deep","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Capstone Software","u":"Capstone Software, GameTek UK Ltd., Ziggurat Interactive","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discoveries_of_the_Deep"},{"t":"Disney's Aladdin","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Platform","r":"November 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disney's_Aladdin_(SNES_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7fan.jpg","s":"Disney's Aladdin is a 1993 video game developed by Capcom for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES). Based on the film of the same name, Disney's Aladdin is a 2D side-scrolling video game in which the player characters are Aladdin and his monkey Abu."},{"t":"Disney's Aladdin","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Virgin Games","u":"Sega","g":"Platformer","r":"October 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disney's_Aladdin_(Genesis_video_game)"},{"t":"Disney's Beauty and the Beast: Belle's Quest","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"Sun Electronics, Tec Toy","g":"Platform","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/disneys-beauty-and-the-beast-belles-quest","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4tg6.jpg","s":"Play as Belle from Disney's \"Beauty and the Beast\" and (loosely) follow the animated film's story. Jump and duck to ward off hindrances, which can come in forms of birds, rats, bats, etc.\n\nAlso have the 'beauty' interact with her supporting characters as you lead her from her start as a village girl, to the final dance with her prince in the ball room."},{"t":"Disney's Chip 'n Dale Rescue Rangers 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom, Make Software","u":"Capcom","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/disneys-chip-n-dale-rescue-rangers-2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3ri5.jpg","s":"Chip and Dale, those tiny but daring detectives, have learned of some terrifying news: The notorious criminal cat has escaped from prison and he's stolen the Urn of the Pharaoh. The Rescue Rangers must once again assemble to help crack the case of the missing urn and return the feline felon, Fat Cat, back to the slammer. Join Chip and Dale and the rest of the Rangers in their wacky new adventure. Help them explore the dangerous levels of the downtown restaurant and meet the exploding personality of Water Rabbit. Aid them on their escape through the freezing floors of the giant refrigerator. Search the ghostly walls of the haunted warehouse and find the final clue to unravel the secret headquarters of Fat Cat."},{"t":"Disney's DuckTales 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Make Software","u":"Capcom, Playtronic","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/disneys-ducktales-2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6sm3.jpg","s":"Scrooge McDuck is hot on the trail of the richest adventure ever. Pieces to the map of the Lost Treasure of McDuck have been scattered across the globe, and Scrooge must find them before the greedy Flintheart Glomgold grabs them. It's going to be a race to the finish as both of them try to find the treasure and become the richest duck in the world! Join Scrooge and his nephews as they explore the ancient pyramids of Egypt and do battle with swash-buckling pirates in the Bermuda Triangle. Help them search the haunted halls of an ancient Scottish castle and scale the watery heights of Niagara Falls. Aid Scrooge in his quest for the secret of the island of Mu and you'll gain the final clue to unravel the hidden location of the Lost Treasure of McDuck!"},{"t":"Disney's Goof Troop","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Puzzle, Adventure","r":"July 11, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/disneys-goof-troop","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co63p7.jpg","s":"Goofy and his son Max were enjoying a peaceful day of fishing, when Keelhaul Pete absconds with Pete and PJ.\n\nNow it's up to Goofy and Max to travel to Spoonerville Island and venture through the jungle to rescue their friends. As either character, you'll take on an army of surly swashbucklers as you dodge exploding cannon balls and search dank dungeons on your way to the final showdown with Keelhaul Pete.\n\nAlong the way, you'll have to dig for treasure and solve puzzles to find the keys that will allow you to enter the pirate's hideout and face Pete."},{"t":"Dogfight","p":["MS-DOS","Atari ST","Amiga"],"d":"MicroProse Leeds","u":"MicroProse","g":"Combat flight simulator","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dogfight_(video_game)"},{"t":"Double Clutch","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"BGS Development","u":"Sega","g":"Racing","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Clutch_(video_game)"},{"t":"Double Switch","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Digital Pictures","u":"Sega, Digital Pictures, Flash Film Works, Screaming Villains","g":"Adventure, interactive movie","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Switch_(video_game)"},{"t":"Dr. Robotnik's Mean Bean Machine","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear"],"d":"Compile","u":"Sega","g":"Falling block puzzle","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dr._Robotnik's_Mean_Bean_Machine","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2v8c.jpg","s":"Dr. Robotnik is changing the jolly folk of Beanville into devious little robots that will help rid Planet Mobius of music and fun forever. Group the beans together to help them escape, and compete against Robotnik’s wily henchbots!"},{"t":"Dracula Unleashed","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Sega CD"],"d":"ICOM Simulations","u":"Viacom New Media, Sega","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dracula_Unleashed"},{"t":"Dragon Ball Z","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Banpresto","g":"Fighting, Strategy, Turn-based strategy (TBS), Arcade","r":"November 1, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/dragon-ball-z","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3u43.jpg","s":"Dragon Ball Z is a fighting game designed and manufactured in Japan by Banpresto in 1993. The game's cabinet is shaped like a robot with markings similar to Goku's gi (suit). The game features large sprites and a color palete that is identical Toriyama's water color scheme in the manga. The environments are semi destructible as chunks of wall or ground could be destroyed. The controls are unique as most of the characters movements are flight related. The playable characters are Goku, Super Saiyan Goku, Gohan, Vegeta, Piccolo, Frieza, Captain Ginyu, Recoome, and Burter."},{"t":"Dragon Ball Z Side Story: Plan to Eradicate the Saiyans","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Ball_Z_Side_Story:_Plan_to_Eradicate_the_Saiyans"},{"t":"Dragon Ball Z: Super Butouden","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"March 20, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/dragon-ball-z-super-butouden--1","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4l0r.jpg","s":"The first fighting game bearing the Dragon Ball license, Super Butouden covers some of the series's key battles (from Goku's battle with Piccolo to the Cell Games)."},{"t":"Dragon Ball Z: Super Butouden 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/dragon-ball-z-super-butouden-2--1","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4l4a.jpg","s":"The second installment of the Super Butoden series focuses on the battles during (and shortly after) the \"Cell Saga\" arc of the Dragon Ball series. It is one of the few games in the series to focus on stories from the series's animated films.\n\nAlthough having a French release, the first time the game was released in North America was when it was distributed as a bonus digital copy for the consumers who pre-ordered Dragon Ball Z: Extreme Butouden. This version was untranslated from Japanese and still with the Japanese title."},{"t":"Dragon Ball Z: Super Butōden","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"March 20, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Ball_Z:_Super_But%C5%8Dden"},{"t":"Dragon Ball Z: Super Butōden 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Ball_Z:_Super_But%C5%8Dden_2"},{"t":"Dragon Quest","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Chunsoft","u":"Enix","g":"Role-playing (RPG)","r":"December 18, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/dragon-quest--3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coaysu.jpg","s":"Remake of Dragon Warrior released for the Super Famicom in the Dragon Quest I.II compilation.\n\nThis remake includes updated graphics that retain the original look, sound of higher quality, and some changes in the gameplay and in the game world: certain locations are larger, some menu commands (such as \"Stairs\") are omitted, and there is a larger variety of items."},{"t":"Dragon Quest I & II","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Quest_I_&_II"},{"t":"Dragon Quest II: Akuryo no Kamigami","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Chunsoft","u":"Enix","g":"Role-playing (RPG), Adventure","r":"December 18, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/dragon-quest-ii-akuryo-no-kamigami","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coaydm.jpg","s":"Remake of Dragon Warrior II released for the Super Famicom in the Dragon Quest I.II compilation.\n\nThis remake includes updated graphics that retain the original look, sound of higher quality, and some changes in the gameplay and in the game world: certain locations are larger, some menu commands (such as \"Stairs\") are omitted, and there is a larger variety of items."},{"t":"Dragon's Revenge","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Tengen","u":"Tengen","g":"Pinball","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon's_Revenge"},{"t":"Dual Orb: Seireiju Densetsu","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dual_Orb:_Seireiju_Densetsu"},{"t":"DuckTales 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Platformer","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DuckTales_2"},{"t":"Duke Nukem II","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software, GT Interactive, Interceptor Entertainment, Gearbox Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"December 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke_Nukem_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1nqa.jpg","s":"Duke Nukem is back. Captured by an alien foe in the middle of promoting his best-selling book, “Why I’m So Great”, Duke must once more defeat the forces of evil to save himself from the knowledge-stealing Rigelatins, and ultimately prove his book’s namesake. In this sequel to the 1991 high-octane, bot-busting video game, Duke Nukem, the King of Action must blast, cannon, missile and flame his way to victory - a victory that would stop the secrets of both ass-kicking and bubblegum from falling into the wrong hands."},{"t":"Dungeon Explorer II","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft, Turbo Technologies Inc.","g":"Action role-playing game, dungeon crawl, hack and slash","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Explorer_II"},{"t":"Dungeon Hack","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"DreamForge Intertainment, Cybelle","u":"Strategic Simulations, Right Stuff","g":"Dungeon crawler","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Hack"},{"t":"Dungeon Master II: The Legend of Skullkeep","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns","Sega CD","Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"FTL Games","u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dungeon_Master_II:_The_Legend_of_Skullkeep","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2yn4.jpg","s":"Dungeon Master established several new standards for role playing and computer games in general. Dungeon Master was a realtime game instead of the traditional turn-based approach that was prevalent until then. Instead of using text-based commands to interact with the environment, players directly manipulated objects and the environment by clicking the mouse in the enlarged first-person view. Abstract Dungeons and Dragons style experience points and levels were eschewed in favor of a system where the characters' skills were improved directly via using them.\n\nIt also introduced some novel control methods including the spell casting system, which involved learning sequences of runes which represented the form and function of a spell's effect. For example, a fireball spell was created by mixing the fire symbol with the wing symbol. This kind of attention to detail and focus on the user interface was typical of the game and helped create an often captivating sense of craft and ingenuity. Other factors in immersiveness were the then-revolutionary use of sound effects to indicate when a creature was nearby, and (primitive) dynamic lighting."},{"t":"Eagle Eye Mysteries","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Stormfront Studios","u":"EA*Kids","g":"Adventure, educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Eye_Mysteries"},{"t":"Eight Ball Deluxe","p":["macOS","MS-DOS"],"d":"LittleWing, P.A.S. Systems","u":"Amtex","g":"Pinball","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eight_Ball_Deluxe_(video_game)"},{"t":"El-Fish","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","FM Towns"],"d":"AnimaTek","u":"Mindscape, Maxis","g":"Life simulation","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El-Fish"},{"t":"Eliminate Down","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Aprinet","u":"Soft Vision","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eliminate_Down"},{"t":"Emeraldia","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Puzzle","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emeraldia"},{"t":"Encarta MindMaze","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encarta_MindMaze"},{"t":"Enma Daiō","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan","u":"Taito","g":"Quiz","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enma_Dai%C5%8D"},{"t":"Epic Pinball","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Digital Extremes","u":"Epic MegaGames","g":"Pinball","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_Pinball"},{"t":"Equinox","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"Sony Imagesoft, Epic/Sony Records","g":"Action adventure, puzzle","r":"November 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equinox_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Eric the Unready","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Legend Entertainment","u":"Legend Entertainment","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_the_Unready"},{"t":"Escape from Monster Manor","p":["3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"Studio 3DO","u":"Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"First-person shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escape_from_Monster_Manor"},{"t":"Eternal Champions","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega Interactive Development Division","u":"Sega","g":"Fighting","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eternal_Champions","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2er8.jpg","s":"Sega released Eternal Champions for their Mega Drive/Genesis in 1993, hoping to capitalize on the fighting game mania that the game industry was in the midst of following the massive success of Street Fighter II (1991) and Mortal Kombat (1992). The game tried to set itself apart with unique features like a heavier emphasis on its story, characters pulled from different time periods, reflectable projectiles, force fields, fighters that carried weapons, a training mode where players had to defend themselves against robotic traps, a novel method of executing moves, and elaborate stage-specific finishing moves called \"Overkills\"."},{"t":"Eye of the Beholder III: Assault on Myth Drannor","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations, Ving Co.","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_the_Beholder_III:_Assault_on_Myth_Drannor","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6k3c.jpg","s":"Eye of the Beholder III : Assault on Myth Drannor is an all 3-D, Legend Series computer role-playing adventure based on the popular Advanced Dungeons & Dragons 2nd Edition rules and on an original story created for this game . The action takes place in and around Myth Drannor, located in TSR's Forgotten Realms game world.\n\nEverything in Eye of the Beholder III is from your point of view . Watch the trees pass by as you move through the forest around Myth Drannor . Pick up items and take them with you. Open doors with keys, by pressing buttons, or by pulling release levers. See monsters draw nearer to you and close in for battle . Attack with the weapons your characters have in their hands. Cast spells with your clerics' or paladins' holy symbols and your mages' spell books . Those in the front of the party may attack with weapons such as swords and maces, while characters in the rear ranks may attack with spells, ranged weapons (such as bows and slings), and polearms."},{"t":"F-1 Sensation","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami, Palcom","g":"Racing","r":"January 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-1_Sensation"},{"t":"F-117 Night Storm","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Electronic Arts","u":"Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Flight simulation","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-117_Night_Storm"},{"t":"F/A-18 Hornet 2.0","p":["macOS"],"d":"Graphic Simulations","u":"Graphic Simulations","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F/A-18_Hornet_2.0"},{"t":"F1","p":["Windows","Atari ST","Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega Master System","Game Gear","Amiga"],"d":"Lankhor, Teque London","u":"Domark","g":"Racing","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_(video_game)"},{"t":"F1 Pole Position 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment, Ubi Soft","g":"Racing","r":"December 24, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_Pole_Position_2"},{"t":"F1 ROC II: Race of Champions","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"SETA","u":"SETA","g":"Racing","r":"March 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_ROC_II:_Race_of_Champions"},{"t":"Fantastic Dizzy","p":["Sega Master System","Game Gear","Genesis / Mega Drive","Amiga"],"d":"Codemasters","u":"Codemasters","g":"Platform, Adventure, Arcade","r":"September 21, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/fantastic-dizzy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2b68.jpg","s":"Dizzy is an egg living in a world of magic and fantasy. The basic concept of the game is to move around the world, finding various items and collectibles, and to use them in the right locations. Dizzy has no special skills or abilities, save for his remarkable feats of somersaulting, and must solve all problems he faces with the clever use of one or more items."},{"t":"Fantasy Empires","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Silicon Knights","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Role-playing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasy_Empires"},{"t":"Fatal Fury Special","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES","PC Engine CD"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK, Magical Company, Takara, Hudson Soft","g":"Fighting","r":"September 16, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatal_Fury_Special","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3b7p.jpg","s":"The GRANDMOTHER of all battles! 15 of the FATAL FURY series' top stars gather in the match of the millenium! Use the resurrected Geese Howard and Wolfgang Krauser in a dazzling arena! All you have ever wanted in a fighting game is here in FATAL FURY SPECIAL!"},{"t":"Fatty Bear's Birthday Surprise","p":["MS-DOS","3DO Interactive Multiplayer","macOS"],"d":"Humongous Entertainment","u":"Humongous Entertainment","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatty_Bear's_Birthday_Surprise"},{"t":"Fields of Glory","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"MicroProse","u":"Spectrum HoloByte, MicroProse","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fields_of_Glory"},{"t":"FIFA International Soccer","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","3DO Interactive Multiplayer","Amiga","MS-DOS","Game Boy","Game Gear","Sega CD","Sega Master System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Extended Play Productions, Creative Assembly, Tiertex, Tec Toy","u":"EA Sports","g":"Sports","r":"December 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_International_Soccer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co207r.jpg","s":"FIFA International Soccer is a 1993 association football video game developed by EA Canada's Extended Play Productions team and published by Electronic Arts. The game was released for the Sega Mega Drive/Genesis console in December 1993 and ported to numerous other systems in 1994. It is the first game in the FIFA series."},{"t":"Fighter's History","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East","g":"Fighting","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fighter's_History_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4cp1.jpg","s":"Fighter's History is a 1993 arcade fighting game developed and published by Data East. It's the inaugural game in the Fighter's History series. The main unique feature of the Fighter's History is its weak point system, which allows the player to temporarily stun an opponent by repeatedly hitting their weak point.\n\nFighter's History became infamous for being the subject of a lawsuit against Data East by Capcom, who claimed the game is too similar to its hit title, Street Fighter II, and as such is copyright infringement on its property. The case was found in Data East's favor however, as the court found that the copied elements were scenes a faire, elements necessary for depicting the scenario. The game was later ported to the Super Nintendo Entertainment System in 1994, and followed by a sequel Karnov's Revenge for Neo Geo.\n\nThere are three different gameplay modes are available. CPU Battle is the traditional arcade mode, where the player select a character and try to complete the game by beating all of the other fighters. In survival mode, the player select a team of five characters to go up against another team. And finally versus mode, where the player can go up against his friends."},{"t":"Final Fight 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"May 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fight_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7pmw.jpg","s":"A beat 'em up sequel to Final Fight, released originally for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System."},{"t":"Final Lap R","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Racing","r":"January 11, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Lap_R"},{"t":"Final Stretch","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Genki","u":"LOZC G. Amusements","g":"Racing","r":"November 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Stretch_(video_game)"},{"t":"Firestriker","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Axes Art Amuse","u":"Hect, DTMC","g":"Action","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firestriker"},{"t":"Flash Hiders","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Right Stuff","u":"Right Stuff","g":"Fighting","r":"December 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flash_Hiders"},{"t":"FlixMix","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Celeris","u":"Celeris, StarCraft","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FlixMix"},{"t":"Fly Harder","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","Commodore 64"],"d":"Starbyte Software","u":"Krisalis Software, Starbyte Software","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fly_Harder"},{"t":"Forever Growing Garden","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Communication Wave","u":"Media Vision","g":"Educational","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forever_Growing_Garden"},{"t":"Forgotten Realms: Unlimited Adventures","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"MicroMagic","u":"Strategic Simulations, U.S. Gold","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgotten_Realms:_Unlimited_Adventures"},{"t":"Freaks!","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freaks!"},{"t":"Freddy Pharkas: Frontier Pharmacist","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freddy_Pharkas:_Frontier_Pharmacist"},{"t":"Front Page Sports Football Pro","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Dynamix","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Front_Page_Sports_Football_Pro"},{"t":"Frontier: Elite II","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"David Braben","u":"GameTek, Konami","g":"Space trading and combat simulator","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frontier:_Elite_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co23pn.jpg","s":"Frontier: Elite 2 is, of course, the sequel to Elite and it continues in much the same way. You have a ship and limited funds, and nowhere to go but out into space. Trade, fight, hunt criminals, work for the various governments, anything goes in Frontier. There are few, if any, limits on what you can do or where you can go. And Frontier's universe (with approx. 100,000,000,000 planets and moons) is so big you'll never see the end of it."},{"t":"Fun 'n Games","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Leland Interactive Media","u":"Tradewest, Sony Electronic Publishing","g":"Edutainment, Maze, Shooter game","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fun_'n_Games"},{"t":"Fury of the Furries","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Atreid Concept","u":"Mindscape","g":"Puzzle-platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fury_of_the_Furries"},{"t":"Gabriel Knight: Sins of the Fathers","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Point-and-click adventure","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_Knight:_Sins_of_the_Fathers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coc236.jpg","s":"The adventure of Gabriel Knight starts with gathering materials for his new book, and ends up becoming a fight for his very soul. During his investigation he discovers that he is the heir to the title of “Schattenjäger”, or “Shadow Hunter”, which has been passed down in his family from generation to generation since times long forgotten. He must now face countless dangers in New Orleans, Africa and Germany, each bringing him ever closer to unraveling the mystery behind suspicious voodoo murders. Haunted by nightmares, he won't give up until he reveals the truth."},{"t":"Gadget: Invention, Travel, & Adventure","p":["FM Towns","macOS"],"d":"Synergy Inc.","u":"Toshiba-EMI, Synergy Interactive, Cryo Interactive Entertainment, NTT Resonant","g":"Adventure, interactive movie","r":"November 28, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gadget:_Invention,_Travel,_&_Adventure"},{"t":"Gaiapolis","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Action role-playing","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaiapolis"},{"t":"Ganbare Goemon 2: Kiteretsu Shōgun McGuiness","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Action-adventure","r":"December 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganbare_Goemon_2:_Kiteretsu_Sh%C5%8Dgun_McGuiness"},{"t":"Gary Grigsby's War in Russia","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Grigsby's_War_in_Russia"},{"t":"Gateway II: Homeworld","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Legend Entertainment","u":"Legend Entertainment","g":"Interactive fiction, Adventure","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gateway_II:_Homeworld"},{"t":"Gateworld","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Homebrew Software","u":"Homebrew Software","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gateworld_(video_game)"},{"t":"Gauntlet IV","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"M2","u":"Tengen","g":"Action","r":"September 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauntlet_IV"},{"t":"Gekitotsu Dangan Jidōsha Kessen: Battle Mobile","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gekitotsu_Dangan_Jid%C5%8Dsha_Kessen:_Battle_Mobile"},{"t":"General Chaos","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Game Refuge","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Chaos","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5lfo.jpg","s":"The game is a quick and dirty battle (or a longer campaign mode that is a series of battles fought over a dynamic strategic map) between two generals, \"General Chaos\" and \"General Havoc\". The objective of the game is to capture the enemy's capital city.\n\nEach player has to pick one of 4 teams, 3 of which have 5 soldiers (with differing selections weapons), while one has 2 \"commandos\" and uses a different control system giving greater control."},{"t":"Gizmos & Gadgets!","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Platform, Puzzle, Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gizmos_&_Gadgets!"},{"t":"Global Domination","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Impressions Games","u":"Impressions Games","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Domination"},{"t":"Globdule","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Ex Animo Designs","u":"Psygnosis","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Globdule"},{"t":"Goblins Quest 3","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Coktel Vision","u":"Sierra Entertainment, Coktel Vision","g":"Point-and-click, Puzzle, Adventure","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/goblins-quest-3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7v8x.jpg","s":"It is said that there is a mysterious labyrinth harboring great secrets that might fulfill the wishes of those who discover them. Two countries respectively ruled by Queen Xina and King Bodd have been competing over the right to explore the labyrinth. However, strange events started happening one after the other: the guardian of the labyrinth died, his daughter disappeared, and King Bodd apparently fell under some sort of a curse. A goblin reporter named Blount get caught in the midst of these events as he attempts to interview the queen and the king. As if that weren't enough, Blount also gets bitten by a werewolf and falls in love, and must find a way to deal with both predicaments.\n\nGoblins Quest is the third entry in the Gobliiins series. Like its predecessors, it is a puzzle adventure game with the emphasis on solving complex tasks confined to smaller areas rather than on exploration. Most levels occupy more than one screen and allow limited navigation between different areas. As the reduction of the title to a single \"i\" implies, this installment only features one controllable goblin. However, at various points of the game Blount will require assistance from other player-controlled characters such as Chump the parrot, Ooya the magician, and Fulbert the snake. In addition, Blount's own spontaneous transformation into a werewolf may affect the solution of some puzzles. The hero will also undergo a few other mutations and drastically change his size during the course of the game."},{"t":"God Medicine","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"July 20, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_Medicine"},{"t":"God of Thunder","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Adept Software","u":"Software Creations","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_of_Thunder_(video_game)"},{"t":"Godzilla: Battle Legends","p":[],"d":"Toho/Alfa System","u":"Toho, Hudson Soft","g":"Fighting","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godzilla:_Battle_Legends"},{"t":"Golden Axe III","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Axe_III","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co28b8.jpg","s":"Chaos is spreading across the land once again as the Prince of Darkness arises to conquer the world. To stop him and retrieve the legendary Golden Axe, four heroes step forward to accept the challenge."},{"t":"Goof Troop","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Action-adventure","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goof_Troop_(video_game)"},{"t":"Gordo 106","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Tenth Planet Software","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Platform","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordo_106"},{"t":"GP-1","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Genki","u":"Atlus","g":"Arcade-style racing","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GP-1"},{"t":"Graham Gooch World Class Cricket","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Audiogenic","u":"Audiogenic, HES","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_Gooch_World_Class_Cricket"},{"t":"Greatest Heavyweights","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Acme Interactive","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_Heavyweights"},{"t":"Grind Stormer","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan, Tengen","u":"Toaplan, Tengen","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grind_Stormer"},{"t":"Ground Zero: Texas","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Digital Pictures","u":"Sony Imagesoft, Sega","g":"Shooter, interactive movie","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ground_Zero:_Texas"},{"t":"Gunnail","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"NMK","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunnail"},{"t":"Gunstar Heroes","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Treasure","u":"Sega","g":"Run and gun","r":"September 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunstar_Heroes","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3wad.jpg","s":"The Gunstar family are the protectors of the planet Gunstar 9. After many years of peace, vicious dictator Colonel Red kidnapped the Gunstar twins’ older brother and used mind control to make him his slave."},{"t":"Gus Goes to Cybertown","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Modern Media Ventures","u":"Modern Media Ventures","g":"Educational, Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gus_Goes_to_Cybertown"},{"t":"Harley's Humongous Adventure","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Visual Concepts","u":"Hi Tech Expressions, Altron","g":"Platform","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harley's_Humongous_Adventure"},{"t":"Haunting","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Electronic Arts","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Action","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haunting_(video_game)"},{"t":"Haunting Starring Polterguy","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Electronic Arts","u":"Electronic Arts, Tec Toy","g":"Simulator","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/haunting-starring-polterguy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5lgx.jpg","s":"Scare the evil, greedy Sardinis out of their houses by turning everyday household objects into something scary, funny or just plain gross. Or use your special spells to really send 'em shrieking. But you gotta hurry, 'cause your ectoplasm may run out! 16 megs of cool graphics, gross special effects and blood-curdling sounds. Unique 3/4 view perspective. Over 400 scary, funny or gross fright items, each causing something different to happen. Five special spells: Zom-B-Ize, Supr-Scare, Boo-Doo, Ecto-Xtra and Dog-Off. \"Killer\" dungeon with 12 different paths!"},{"t":"Hebereke's Popoon","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Windows"],"d":"Sunsoft","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebereke's_Popoon"},{"t":"Hell Cab","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Digital Fusion","u":"Time Warner","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell_Cab"},{"t":"Hi-Ten Bomberman","p":[],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Action, maze","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hi-Ten_Bomberman"},{"t":"Hired Guns","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"DMA Design","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hired_Guns"},{"t":"Hlava Kasandry","p":["Atari ST"],"d":"LetDisk","g":"Adventure, RPG, simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hlava_Kasandry"},{"t":"Hollywood Mogul","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Carey DeVuono","u":"Hollywood Mogul Company","g":"Business Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hollywood_Mogul"},{"t":"Homey D. Clown","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Synergistic Software","u":"Capstone Software","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homey_D._Clown_(video_game)"},{"t":"Hutsell Computer War Games","p":[],"d":"W. R. Hutsell","g":"Wargames","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hutsell_Computer_War_Games"},{"t":"Hyper Duel","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Technosoft","u":"Taito","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyper_Duel"},{"t":"Ihatovo Monogatari","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hector","u":"Hector","g":"Adventure","r":"March 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ihatovo_Monogatari"},{"t":"Illusion of Gaia","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Quintet","u":"Enix, Nintendo","g":"Action role-playing","r":"November 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illusion_of_Gaia","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8kyi.jpg","s":"Journey into the dark side of an ancient civilization.\n\nRadiating an ominous light in its path, the Chaos Comet threatens the existence of the planet. Battles erupt and a tentative peace is returned to the lands. Danger lurks as the Chaos Comet orbits and dreadful beasts lie awaiting.\n\nIn the Age of Exploration a boy named Will is born. Tragedy strikes during an expedition to the Tower of Babel separating Will from his father. Telekinetic forces awaken as Will returns to South Cape. Armed with his transformation abilities of two great warriors, Will must save the planet.\n\nGaia, Will's guiding spirit bestows magical powers and advice along his journey. With the help of his friends Will must battle relentless enemies and solve the puzzles of the ancient ruins. Patience and knowledge are essential to reunite this father and son."},{"t":"In the Hunt","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem, Xing Entertainment, Kokopeli Digital Studios, Imagineer","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Hunt","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1uc0.jpg","s":"In the Hunt is a horizontal scrolling shooter arcade game by Irem. It was released in 1993 and later ported to the Sega Saturn, the PlayStation and Windows 95 for the PC by Kokopeli."},{"t":"Inca II: Wiracocha","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Coktel Vision","u":"Sierra Online","g":"Adventure game, Space combat simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inca_II:_Wiracocha"},{"t":"IndyCar Racing","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Papyrus Design Group","u":"Papyrus, Virgin Interactive","g":"Sim racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IndyCar_Racing"},{"t":"Innocent Until Caught","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Divide By Zero","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innocent_Until_Caught"},{"t":"Inspector Gadget","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"AIM","u":"Hudson Soft USA","g":"2D action platformer","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inspector_Gadget_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5osj.jpg","s":"Inspector Gadget is on yet another mission against the evil Doctor Claw and his army of M.A.D. (Mean And Dirty) agents. This time it's personal because Doctor Claw has captured Gadget's niece, Penny. With the help of his trusty dog, Brain, and his suit full of special gadgets, Inspector Gadget must save Penny and defeat Doctor Claw and his minions!"},{"t":"International Rugby Challenge","p":["Amiga","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Domark, Tiertex","u":"Domark","g":"Sports","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Rugby_Challenge"},{"t":"Investigating Lake Iluka","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Interactive Multimedia Learning Laboratory","u":"Interactive Multimedia Pty Ltd","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Investigating_Lake_Iluka"},{"t":"Iron Helix","p":["Windows","macOS","Sega CD"],"d":"Drew Pictures","u":"Spectrum HoloByte","g":"Adventure / Survival horror","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Helix"},{"t":"Ishar 2: Messengers of Doom","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Silmarils","u":"Silmarils, Daze Marketing","g":"Role-playing game","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishar_2:_Messengers_of_Doom"},{"t":"Isle of the Dead","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Rainmaker Software","u":"Merit Software","g":"First-person shooter, point-and-click adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_the_Dead_(video_game)"},{"t":"J.F.K. Assassination: A Visual Investigation","p":[],"u":"Kozel Multimedia","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J.F.K._Assassination:_A_Visual_Investigation"},{"t":"James Pond 3","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Amiga","Amiga CD32","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Gear"],"d":"Vectordean","u":"Millennium Interactive, Electronic Arts, U.S. Gold","g":"Platformer","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Pond_3"},{"t":"Jetpack","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Adam Pedersen","u":"Software Creations","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetpack_(video_game)"},{"t":"Jim Power: The Lost Dimension in 3-D","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Loriciel, Piko Interactive","u":"Loriciel, Electro Brain, Piko Interactive","g":"Platform","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Power:_The_Lost_Dimension_in_3-D"},{"t":"Jimmy Connors Tennis","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Atari Lynx"],"d":"NMS Software, Hand Made Software","u":"Ubi Soft, Atari Corporation","g":"Tennis","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_Connors_Tennis"},{"t":"Jonny Quest: Curse of the Mayan Warriors","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Presage Software","u":"Hollyware Entertainment","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonny_Quest:_Curse_of_the_Mayan_Warriors"},{"t":"Joy Mech Fight","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo R&D1","u":"Nintendo","g":"Fighting","r":"May 21, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joy_Mech_Fight"},{"t":"Jungle Strike","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"High Score Productions, Granite Bay Software","u":"Electronic Arts, Ocean Software, Black Pearl Software, Gremlin Interactive","g":"Shoot 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jungle_Strike","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5muv.jpg","s":"A sequel to Desert Strike: Return to the Gulf (a best-seller released the previous year) and the second instalment in the Strike series. Jungle Strike retained its predecessor's core mechanics and expanded on the model with additional vehicles and settings. The game was well received by most critics upon release, with the Mega Drive release receiving critical acclaim from their respective reviewers. Publications praised its gameplay, strategy, design, controls and graphics, although there were some complaints regarding the interface and difficulty."},{"t":"Jurassic Park","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Ocean Software","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Action","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_(computer_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2zdt.jpg","s":"Jurassic Park is a SEGA Genesis/Mega Drive game based on the first movie.\nThe game was playable as either Dr. Alan Grant or a Velociraptor (presumably the The Big One). Grant is the default character but can be changed using the 'Player' option on the game's main menu to swich to the raptor."},{"t":"Jurassic Park","p":["Game Gear","Sega Master System"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"July 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_(Game_Gear_video_game)"},{"t":"Jurassic Park","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Ocean Software","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Action","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_(NES_video_game)"},{"t":"Jurassic Park","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"BlueSky Software","u":"Sega","g":"Action-adventure, platform","r":"August 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_(Sega_Genesis_video_game)"},{"t":"Jurassic Park","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Ocean of America","u":"Ocean Software, Jaleco","g":"Action-adventure","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_(SNES_video_game)"},{"t":"Jyutei Senki","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"TamTam","u":"Enix Corporation","g":"Strategy involving breeding/construction","r":"August 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jyutei_Senki"},{"t":"Kaijū-ō Godzilla","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Bandai","u":"Bandai","g":"Action","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaij%C5%AB-%C5%8D_Godzilla"},{"t":"Kart Fighter","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hummer Team","u":"Ge De Industry Co.","g":"Fighting","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kart_Fighter"},{"t":"Kasparov's Gambit","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Heuristic Software","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Chess","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasparov's_Gambit"},{"t":"Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Park Place Productions","u":"GameTek","g":"Racing with top-down perspective","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kawasaki_Caribbean_Challenge"},{"t":"Keio Flying Squadron","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Victor Entertainment","u":"JVC Musical Industries","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"August 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keio_Flying_Squadron"},{"t":"Ken's Labyrinth","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Ken Silverman","u":"Epic MegaGames","g":"First-person shooter","r":"January 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken's_Labyrinth"},{"t":"Kendo Rage","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Affect","u":"Datam Polystar, SETA","g":"Action game, platformer","r":"January 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kendo_Rage"},{"t":"Kero Kero Keroppi no Daibouken","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kero_Kero_Keroppi_no_Daibouken"},{"t":"Kether","p":["CD-i"],"d":"Infogrames","u":"Philips","g":"Space flight simulation, adventure","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kether_(video_game)"},{"t":"Kevin Keegan's Player Manager","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Anco Software","u":"Imagineer","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Keegan's_Player_Manager"},{"t":"Kid Dracula","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platformer","r":"January 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kid_Dracula_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6vv9.jpg","s":"His pearly fangs drive the girls wild, while his dead-of-the-party sense of humor makes them scream. He's Kid Dracula, star of the mythical Transylvania 92010, and the coolest living corpse ever to stake his claim on the Game Boy. If you thought Mario had a way with magic, you should see junior jugular's spellbinding powers, including the mesmerizing bat transformation. Poof! Nobody wings it better than Kid Drac.\n\nThis game is both a remake and a sequel to the original Kid Dracula, on the Nintendo Entertainment System."},{"t":"Kingmaker","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"TM Games","u":"Avalon Hill, U.S. Gold","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingmaker_(video_game)"},{"t":"Kirby's Adventure","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"March 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirby's_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coba3x.jpg","s":"Not everything is well in Dream Land. For some mysterious reason, the Dream Spring, a magical well that is the reservoir for all the dreams of the citizens of Dream Land, is no longer working. Everyone is being subjected to their worst nightmares every time they go to sleep. Upon reaching the Dream Spring, Kirby finds out that evil King Dedede has stolen the Star Rod, thus depriving Dream Land of the magical energy that feeds its spring. Using 20 unique tricks and your ability to steal your enemies' powers by swallowing them, you'll have to make your way through a horrific land filled with all kinds of nightmares. Recover the broken pieces of the Star Rod, and everyone in Dream Land will sleep peacefully once again. If you fail, the citizens of Dream Land will be subjected to a lifetime of terrible nightmares."},{"t":"Kirby's Pinball Land","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"Nintendo","g":"Pinball","r":"November 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirby's_Pinball_Land","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4qv2.jpg","s":"Choose your world and take aim! Kirby will be launched into the fast-paced danger of uncharted worlds, but you can help him face his foes head on. It's a pinball race to check out each new world's surprise enemies and pitfalls. Flip Kirby in battles against lethal eyeballs, a shooting tree and mace-swinging maniacs. Take aim and leap to new worlds!"},{"t":"Knuckle Bash","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan","u":"Toaplan, Atari Games","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knuckle_Bash"},{"t":"Knuckle Heads","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Fighting","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knuckle_Heads"},{"t":"Krazy Ace Miniature Golf","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Telegames","u":"Telegames","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krazy_Ace_Miniature_Golf"},{"t":"Kunio-kun no Dodgeball da yo Zen'in Shūgō","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kunio-kun_no_Dodgeball_da_yo_Zen'in_Sh%C5%ABg%C5%8D"},{"t":"Kunio-kun's Nekketsu Soccer League","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Technos Japan","u":"Technos Japan","g":"Fighting, Sport, Hack and slash/Beat 'em up","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/kunio-kuns-nekketsu-soccer-league","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7xuv.jpg","s":"Hi, I am Misako of Nekketsu High. I am the manager of our local soccer team. Finally, we can prove that Nekketsu FC is the greatest team in the world. If we work hard and qualify, the Technos Japan Cup will determine who is the greatest in the world. After Nekketsu FC was formed, I was supposed to lead the team to victory, but I haven't been able to keep my promise. But Kunio is here, and he will guide us to victory. I won't meddle in, but I will offer my advice if you want it. Nekketsu FC, let's go!\n\nThe game was originally released as Kunio-Kun no Nekketsu Soccer League, but has since been localized as Kunio-kun's Nekketsu Soccer League."},{"t":"Land of Illusion Starring Mickey Mouse","p":["Game Gear","Sega Master System"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_of_Illusion_Starring_Mickey_Mouse","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4zmt.jpg","s":"Mickey finds himself in a fairy tale land where an evil Phantom-in-the-Clouds has stolen a special gem which brought peace and prosperity to a tiny village. It's up to you to help him save the day - and earn a place in Minnie's heart!"},{"t":"Lands of Lore: The Throne of Chaos","p":["MS-DOS","FM Towns","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Westwood Studios","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Role-playing, dungeon crawl","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lands_of_Lore:_The_Throne_of_Chaos","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1y7r.jpg","s":"Lands of Lore: The Throne of Chaos takes you on an adventure: save the dying king and stop the evil sorceress Scotia’s Dark Army. Your band of heroes will travel through a vast fantasy word full of mysterious places, dangerous monsters, and hidden treasures in a quest to find a way to defeat the immortal evil enchantress and save the world."},{"t":"Last Action Hero","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Game Gear"],"d":"Teeny Weeny Games, Bits Studios, The Dome","u":"Sony Imagesoft, Psygnosis","g":"Action, beat em up","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_Action_Hero_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4odq.jpg","s":"Last Action Hero is a series of action video games based on the film of the same name. Versions were released for the NES, SNES, Sega Genesis, Game Boy, Sega Game Gear, Amiga and MS-DOS. Versions for the Sega Master System and Sega CD were also announced, but never released."},{"t":"Leisure Suit Larry 6: Shape Up or Slip Out!","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leisure_Suit_Larry_6:_Shape_Up_or_Slip_Out!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2xnz.jpg","s":"Shape Up or Slip Out! is the fifth game in Al Lowe's Leisure Suit Larry series. All the events from the previous games are forgotten. Larry is once again single, and again his goal is to capture the interest of various ladies. By sheer coincidence, Larry finds himself on a TV Game Show called \"Stallions\". He wins a weekend at the Spa Resort, La Costa Lotta. Naturally, there are some beautiful women in the spa who are just dreaming about such a wonderful, handsome man as Larry! Uh... or is it really so? It is the player's goal to find out!\n\nThis instalment focuses more on conversations with women and puzzles than on the storyline. The entire game is spent at La Costa Lotta. There are plenty of traditional inventory-based puzzles to solve in the game. Larry can die, but the player is allowed to try again every time that happens, without the need to restore a saved game. This is also the first Larry game with SVGA graphics, and voice-overs in the CD version."},{"t":"Lemmings 2: The Tribes","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Acorn Archimedes","FM Towns"],"d":"DMA Design","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Puzzle","r":"February 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemmings_2:_The_Tribes","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2hgz.jpg","s":"By now, you'll probably be aware of the horrible plight the Lemmings face. Not only do they have to leave their lovely little hovels for good,but they also have to escape their island before they perish in the Darkness that's coming. Your objective therefore is to save as many Lemmings as possible from each tribe and help them escape the Island. The DOS version has a different sounding soundtrack due to the different sound card, but is otherwise identical."},{"t":"Liberation: Captive 2","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"Byte Engineers","u":"Mindscape","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation:_Captive_2"},{"t":"Liberty or Death","p":["PC-9800 series","MS-DOS"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"September 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_or_Death_(video_game)"},{"t":"Link: The Faces of Evil","p":["CD-i"],"d":"Animation Magic","u":"Philips Interactive Media","g":"Adventure","r":"October 10, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/link-the-faces-of-evil","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co60ta.jpg","s":"Link: The Faces of Evil was the product of a compromise between Nintendo and Philips following their failure to release a CD-ROM based add-on to the Super Nintendo Entertainment System. The Faces of Evil differ from most conventional Zelda games as it is a platformer with a side-scrolling view, similar to The Adventure of Link. Stages are accessed from a world map, with more becoming available as Link clears an area or defeats a boss. The game have been subject to much criticism and Nintendo does not recognize it as part of the series."},{"t":"Link: The Faces of Evil and Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon","p":["CD-i"],"d":"Animation Magic","u":"Philips Interactive Media","g":"Action-adventure","r":"October 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Link:_The_Faces_of_Evil_and_Zelda:_The_Wand_of_Gamelon"},{"t":"Lionheart","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Thalion Software","u":"Thalion Software","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lionheart_(video_game)"},{"t":"Litil Divil","p":["MS-DOS","CD-i"],"d":"Gremlin Graphics","u":"Gremlin Graphics, Philips Interactive Media, Rushware, M.C.M. Software","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Litil_Divil"},{"t":"Lords of Thunder","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Red Company","u":"Turbo Technologies, Hudson Soft","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lords_of_Thunder"},{"t":"Lost in Time","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Coktel Vision","u":"Coktel Vision, Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_in_Time_(video_game)"},{"t":"Lost Secret of the Rainforest","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line, Coktel Vision","g":"Educational, adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_Secret_of_the_Rainforest"},{"t":"Lucky & Wild","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"First-person racing/shooter","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucky_&_Wild"},{"t":"Lufia & the Fortress of Doom","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Neverland","u":"Taito","g":"Role-playing","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lufia_&_the_Fortress_of_Doom","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobu07.jpg","s":"The Sinsitrals, four masters of Doom, arrived in this land on their floating island 100 years ago. You will begin your quest by travelling through time to join in a temendous battle against them--and you will determine the outcome. When you return to present time, you and Lufia, your companion, must join forces with two others to prevent the Sinistrals from regaining control of your world. It will be a long and difficult task--This land is larger than that of most RPG games. And in the end, the balance of fate will be overturned in a contest of love and betrayal that could destroy you all."},{"t":"Lunicus","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Cyberflix","u":"Paramount Interactive","g":"Adventure","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunicus"},{"t":"Mad Shark","p":["Arcade"],"d":"MOSS","u":"Allumer","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mad_Shark"},{"t":"Madden NFL '94","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"High Score Productions, Visual Concepts","u":"EA Sports, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Sports","r":"November 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madden_NFL_'94"},{"t":"Magic Boy","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Blue Turtle","u":"Empire Interactive, JVC Musical Industries","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic_Boy_(video_game)"},{"t":"Major Stryker","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_Stryker"},{"t":"Malibu Bikini Volleyball","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Hand Made Software","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Sports","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malibu_Bikini_Volleyball"},{"t":"Man Enough","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Tsunami Media","u":"Tsunami Media","g":"Dating sim","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man_Enough"},{"t":"Mansion of Hidden Souls","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"System Sacom","u":"Sega, Vic Tokai","g":"Adventure","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansion_of_Hidden_Souls_(Sega_CD_video_game)"},{"t":"Mario & Wario","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Game Freak","u":"Nintendo","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"August 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_&_Wario","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co244d.jpg","s":"Mario & Wario is a puzzle game that is compatible with the SNES Mouse. The player chooses Princess Peach, Mario or Yoshi, and Wario proceeds to drop a bucket on their head (the object changes in later levels). The player controls Wanda the fairy to guide the now-blinded character through an obstacle course, to reach Luigi waiting at the end."},{"t":"Mario Is Missing!","p":["MS-DOS","Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"The Software Toolworks","u":"The Software Toolworks, Mindscape","g":"Educational","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Is_Missing!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co21l6.jpg","s":"Mario Is Missing! is an educational game and the first game where Luigi is the main protagonist with Mario in a supporting role. The main gameplay consists of moving around a city in side-scrolling manner while jumping on Koopas to collect stolen artifacts. Luigi then must secure the city by taking these artifacts to their respective locations and correctly answering two questions about the landmark.The computer version is a point-and-click adventure game, while the NES and SNES versions have more of an emphasis on platforming."},{"t":"Mario's Early Years!","p":["MS-DOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"The Software Toolworks","u":"The Software Toolworks, Mindscape","g":"Edutainment","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario's_Early_Years!"},{"t":"Mario's Time Machine","p":["MS-DOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"The Software Toolworks, Radical Entertainment, Knowledge Adventure","u":"The Software Toolworks","g":"Educational","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario's_Time_Machine","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1wwm.jpg","s":"Mario's Time Machine is an edutainment title and the successor of Mario is Missing! The game is meant to teach younger players basic world history and is the last Mario Discovery Series game before discontinuation. This game and Hotel Mario are the only games in the Mario franchise in which Mario as a protagonist has full lines of dialogue. The objective of the game is to return the historical artifacts to their proper time periods thanks to the central hub. As soon as you enter a certain time period you need to explore and interact with various residents of the area in order to get more information about the artifact, the period itself and the person connected to it."},{"t":"Martial Champion","p":["Arcade","PC Engine","PC Engine CD"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Fighting","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martial_Champion"},{"t":"Master of Orion","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Simtex, Take-Two","u":"MicroProse","g":"Turn-based strategy, 4X","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Master_of_Orion","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4ejt.jpg","s":"Master of Orion is a competitive game of interstellar conquest that combines exploration with conflict. You are cast as the immortal emperor who shapes the future of your race, as contact is made with the neighbouring races. Your objective is simple: control a majority of the known galaxy and eliminate all who stand in the way.\n\nAs ruler you must ultimately decide the destiny of your race as you make decisions on how planetary resources are allocated, where star fleets will be deployed, which races to fight, and which races to ally with. You begin with control of your home planet, from which you can explore and colonize nearby star systems. Your first decisions will centre around the rapid development of colonies into productive worlds, what types of technology to focus on, and which star systems to colonize.\n\nHowever, the true challenge begins when contact is made with other races, and complex strategies must be formulated to manage diplomacy, sabotage, espionage, trade, and interstellar combat."},{"t":"Math Blaster Episode I: In Search of Spot","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Davidson & Associates","u":"Davidson & Associates","g":"Educational","r":"September 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Math_Blaster_Episode_I:_In_Search_of_Spot"},{"t":"Math Rabbit","p":["Apple II","MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Math_Rabbit"},{"t":"Mayhem in Monsterland","p":["Commodore 64"],"d":"Apex Computer Productions","u":"Apex Computer Productions","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayhem_in_Monsterland"},{"t":"Mazin Saga: Mutant Fighter","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Almanic Corporation","u":"Sega, Vic Tokai","g":"Beat 'em up, fighting","r":"February 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mazin_Saga:_Mutant_Fighter"},{"t":"Mazinger Z","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Winkysoft","u":"Bandai","g":"Action, platformer","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mazinger_Z_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"McDonald's Treasure Land Adventure","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Treasure","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"September 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McDonald's_Treasure_Land_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2qnq.jpg","s":"McDonald's Treasure Land Adventure is a side-scrolling adventure starring Ronald McDonald.\n\nAfter finding a piece of a treasure map, the yellow clown travels through four different levels (Forest, Town, Sea and Moon). He's off to find and defeat the baddies who have the other pieces of the map.\n\nRonald can jump, shoot and use his scarf to grab onto hooks to jump to higher levels.\n\nJewels and flowers collected in each level can be used to purchase extra lives and power-ups in shops between levels."},{"t":"MechWarrior","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Activision, Victor Musical Industries","g":"Sci-fi mech simulation","r":"February 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MechWarrior_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Mega Man 6","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"November 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_6","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co55cf.jpg","s":"From the United States, Canada and Japan they came. The world's premier designers and their finest robotic warriors traveled to do battle in the First Annual Robot Tournament. But what began as a game suddenly took a terrifying twist! On the eve of the Grand Championship, the sponsor of the event announced that the entire tournament was just an elaborate scheme to get his hands on the world's most powerful robots! Now faced with an army of metallic mercenaries, Mega Man must fight a ferocious new foe - The Mysterious Mr. X!"},{"t":"Mega Man IV","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Minakuchi Engineering","u":"Capcom","g":"Platform","r":"October 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_IV_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co62v1.jpg","s":"A huge celebration at the World Robot Expo is shattered by the sight of a flying saucer streaking across the sky. But instead of holding little green men, this saucer holds one very mad scientist! Armed with a mind control machine that transmits a sinister signal. Dr. Wily is ready to mesmerize all the robots of the city into an army of mindless metallic mercenaries!\n\nBattling the effects of Wily's mind control machine, Mega Man and his robotic reenforcements, Rush and Beat must blast to molten metal an army of foes led by eight of Wily's greatest robot masters! And should Mega Man survive, Dr. Wily is waiting to unleash the heavy artillery with his latest creation, the awesome Wily Tank!"},{"t":"Mega Man X","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_X_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3tu2.jpg","s":"Mega Man X, known as Rockman X in Japan, is the first game of the Mega Man X series. It was the first spin-off of the classic Mega Man series and made its debut on the Super Famicom/SNES, initially released in 1993. It was ported to the PC in 1995, and to Android and iOS devices in 2011. It was re-released as part of Mega Man X Collection in January 2006, then later re-released in Mega Man X Legacy Collection in July 2018. The game takes place in the year 21XX (early 22nd century), a century after the conclusion of the classic Mega Man series, and stars a new main character named X."},{"t":"Mega Turrican","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Factor 5, Kaiko/Neon Studios","u":"Data East, Sony Imagesoft, Sega, Rainbow Arts/Renegade Software","g":"Run and gun","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Turrican","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2l0s.jpg","s":"The Machine has enslaved the galaxy under its tyrannical rule. As Bren McGuire, the last survivor of the United Freedom Forces, it is your duty to destroy the evil forces of the Machine that have devastated the galaxy."},{"t":"MegaRace","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Cryo","u":"The Software Toolworks","g":"Vehicular combat","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MegaRace"},{"t":"Metal & Lace: The Battle of the Robo Babes","p":["FM Towns","MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Forest","u":"Forest, Megatech Software","g":"Fighting, eroge","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_&_Lace:_The_Battle_of_the_Robo_Babes"},{"t":"Metal Combat: Falcon's Revenge","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Intelligent Systems","u":"Nintendo","g":"Light-gun shooter","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Combat:_Falcon's_Revenge"},{"t":"Metal Marines","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Namco, Mindscape","u":"Namco, Mindscape","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Marines"},{"t":"Metal Max 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Crea-Tech, Data East","u":"Data East, Now Production, Kadokawa Games","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"March 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Max_2"},{"t":"Metamor Jupiter","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Flight-Plan","u":"NEC","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metamor_Jupiter"},{"t":"Metamorphic Force","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metamorphic_Force"},{"t":"Michael Jordan in Flight","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Electronic Arts","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Jordan_in_Flight"},{"t":"Mickey Mouse: Magic Wands!","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Kemco","u":"Kemco, Nintendo","g":"Puzzle, action","r":"December 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mickey_Mouse:_Magic_Wands!"},{"t":"Mickey's Safari in Letterland","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Edutainment, platformer","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mickey's_Safari_in_Letterland","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6oqx.jpg","s":"Endlessly entertaining, MICKEY'S SAFARI IN LETTERLAND is a delightful way to help children improve important early recognition skills as they have fun with Mickey and his Disney pal, Goofy! 6 Exciting animated worlds of adventure await all who join Mickey on his safari to find the lost letter tablets and return them to the museum. Hidden surprises are scattered throughout each level--including rare \"alphabet jewels\" that spell out words and add extra challenge to the fun."},{"t":"Microcosm","p":["FM Towns","Sega CD"],"d":"Psygnosis","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Rail shooter","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcosm_(video_game)"},{"t":"Microsoft Arcade","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Microsoft","u":"Microsoft","g":"Various","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Arcade"},{"t":"Microsoft Dinosaurs","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Dinosaurs"},{"t":"Microsoft Flight Simulator 5.0","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Bruce Artwick Organization","u":"Microsoft","g":"Flight simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Flight_Simulator_5.0"},{"t":"MiG-29: Fighter Pilot","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"PanelComp, The Kremlin","u":"Domark","g":"Combat flight simulation","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MiG-29:_Fighter_Pilot"},{"t":"Might and Magic V: Darkside of Xeen","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","FM Towns","PC-9800 series"],"d":"New World Computing","u":"New World Computing","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Might_and_Magic_V:_Darkside_of_Xeen"},{"t":"Mighty Final Fight","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"June 11, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mighty_Final_Fight","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3c4b.jpg","s":"The Mad Gear Gang has spread crime and violence throughout the streets of Metro City. Only one man has enough guts to take the streets back from them, Mayor Mike Haggar. Now the Mad Gear Gang has kidnapped his daughter Jessica to bring him under their control. But they've made one mistake: Haggar's furious and he wants someone's head! Guy and Jessica's boyfriend, Cody, are joining Haggar to clean up the slime-filled streets and rescue Jessica. Along the way you'll find food to replenish your health, or better yet, search for weapons to knock the living daylights out of your enemies. So join Haggar and his friends as they try to free the city from the evil clutches of the Mad Gear Gang!"},{"t":"Miracle Girls","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miracle_Girls"},{"t":"Monopoly","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monopoly_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Monster Bash","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform","r":"April 9, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_Bash"},{"t":"Monster Maulers","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Fighting game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_Maulers"},{"t":"More Vegas Games","p":["Windows"],"d":"The Dreamers Guild","u":"New World Computing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/More_Vegas_Games"},{"t":"Morph","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"Millennium Interactive","u":"Sony Imagesoft","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morph_(video_game)"},{"t":"Morphman","p":["Windows"],"d":"Dynamic Dimension Development, Inc.","u":"Dynamic Dimension Development, Inc.","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morphman"},{"t":"Motoko-chan no Wonder Kitchen","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Ajinomoto","u":"Ajinomoto","g":"Simulation","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motoko-chan_no_Wonder_Kitchen"},{"t":"Mr. Nutz","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Ocean, Planet Interactive Development, DreamOn Studio","u":"Ocean, Infogrames","g":"Platform","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mr._Nutz","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co39sy.jpg","s":"You are the cool squirrel of the title, and you must defeat the evil Yeti before he freezes the entire world. Your quest will make you explore many areas such as a forest, a witch's cottage, a volcano, the clouds, a circus...\n\nMr. Nutz is a platform game, in which Nutz must jump on enemies to kill them. His tail can also be controlled. You move through levels using ledges, ladders, bridges and platforms. There are some simple puzzles involving object manipulation."},{"t":"Mutant League Football","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Mutant Productions","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Sports","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutant_League_Football"},{"t":"Muzeum Mrtvol","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Computer Experts","u":"Vochozka Trading","g":"Text adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muzeum_Mrtvol"},{"t":"Myst","p":["macOS"],"d":"Cyan","u":"Broderbund, Sunsoft, Acclaim Entertainment, SoftBank","g":"Adventure","r":"September 24, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myst","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1y96.jpg","s":"Myst is a graphic adventure puzzle game developed by Cyan and released in 1993.\nPlayers explore a mysterious island filled with interactive environments, books, and clues that reveal its story. The gameplay focuses on solving logic-based puzzles and uncovering the fate of two trapped brothers. It features pre-rendered visuals and a non-linear structure that encourages exploration and observation.\n\nMyst became one of the best-selling PC games of its era and influenced the adventure game genre."},{"t":"Mystery at the Museums","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Binary Zoo Software","u":"Artech Studios","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mystery_at_the_Museums"},{"t":"Mystic Formula","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Microcabin","u":"Microcabin","g":"Run and gun","r":"July 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mystic_Formula"},{"t":"Mystic Warriors","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Run and gun","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mystic_Warriors"},{"t":"Nadia: The Secret of Blue Water","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","PC-9800 series","Sharp X68000","PC Engine CD","FM Towns"],"d":"List, Advance Communication Company, Namco, Gainax","u":"List, Toho, Namco, Gainax","g":"Various","r":"January 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nadia:_The_Secret_of_Blue_Water_(video_game)"},{"t":"NBA Showdown","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"EA Creative Development","u":"EA Sports","g":"Sports","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_Showdown_(video_game)"},{"t":"NetWars","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NetWars"},{"t":"Network Q RAC Rally","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series","FM Towns"],"d":"Pixelkraft","u":"Europress, Accolade, Victor Entertainment","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Q_RAC_Rally_(video_game)"},{"t":"NFL Football '94 Starring Joe Montana","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"BlueSky Software","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_Football_'94_Starring_Joe_Montana"},{"t":"NFL Quarterback Club","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Iguana Entertainment, Beam Software, Condor","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_Quarterback_Club_(video_game)"},{"t":"NHL '94","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"High Score Productions, EA Canada, Park Place Productions, Empty Clip Studios","u":"EA Sports, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Sports","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NHL_'94","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2gvn.jpg","s":"Aside from the licenses, the gameplay for this edition sees for the first time the inclusion of the devastating one-timer shot, but to keep forwards (literally) in check, defenders also feature harder body checks, with new animations. For an additional challenge, players can also choose to control the goalkeeper manually. A popular feature in previous editions, fighting is completely removed from the game. Game modes include exhibition games (or \"regular season\" in-game), playoffs (best of one or best of seven) and an all new shoot-out mode. The main options remain basically the same couple since the establishment of the series - period length (5, 10 or 20 minutes), line changes (off, manual or the new automatic mode) and penalties. (off, on, on but no offsides)."},{"t":"NHL Stanley Cup","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Nintendo","g":"Sports","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NHL_Stanley_Cup_(video_game)"},{"t":"Night Slashers","p":["Arcade","Windows"],"d":"Data East, Storm Trident","u":"Data East, Forever Entertainment","g":"Beat 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_Slashers"},{"t":"Nightwalker: The Midnight Detective","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nightwalker:_The_Midnight_Detective"},{"t":"Ninja Baseball Bat Man","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Baseball_Bat_Man","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7m4z.jpg","s":"Ninja Baseball Bat Man is a 1993 beat 'em up developed and published by Irem Corporation in association with its North American division Irem America exclusively as an arcade game. It is the fourth arcade game by IREM to use a belt scroll perspective, following Blade Master, Hook and Undercover Cops."},{"t":"Nomad","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Intense! Interactive, Papyrus Design Group","u":"GameTek","g":"Space trading and combat","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nomad_(video_game)"},{"t":"Nostradamus","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Face","u":"Face","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nostradamus_(video_game)"},{"t":"Numan Athletics","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Sports","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numan_Athletics"},{"t":"Ogre Battle: The March of the Black Queen","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Quest Corporation","u":"Quest Corporation, Enix America, Riverhillsoft, Artdink","g":"Tactical role-playing, real-time strategy","r":"March 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ogre_Battle:_The_March_of_the_Black_Queen","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co634z.jpg","s":"It’s up to you to defeat the Empire. It’s been nearly a quarter of a century since the Zetegenian Empire first conquered the Kingdoms of Zenobia with a wrath of fear and bloodshed.\n\nYou are the leader of a band of rebels who’ve fought to preserve the last shred of honor in this desperate time of treachery. Manage the ranks of a full-blown rebel army, complete with hundreds of characters, magic items, weapons and mystical Tarot cards. You must succeed in ousting the evil usurpers - your fate, and that of the entire population, depends on it."},{"t":"Onita Atsushi FMW","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Marionette","u":"Pony Canyon","g":"Wrestling","r":"August 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onita_Atsushi_FMW"},{"t":"Operation Europe: Path to Victory","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","PC-9800 series","FM Towns"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"January 16, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Europe:_Path_to_Victory"},{"t":"Operation Logic Bomb","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Action","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Logic_Bomb","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4ik8.jpg","s":"The best scientists in the world have been working for years on the Subspace Particle Transfer Project, a process that uses subspace transporters to dematerialize an object, then instantly rematerializes it at another location. Short distance transfers of individuals have also been successful, and the scientists were about to begin long-range transport experiments.\n\nHowever, contact with the research laboratory has been lost. Someone - or something - has taken over the facility and shut down all outside communications. There is no information about the intruders or their capabilities... no one has entered the facility and returned to tell about it. Plans for a final assault have been prepared and if it fails, there will be no choice but to destroy the entire installation. Agent Logan, who has been fitted with bioelectronic implants that give him superior strength, reflexes, and anlystical abilities, has been briefed on all aspects of the mission. Now it's up to him to infiltrate the lab, neutralize the intruders, and rescure the scientists."},{"t":"Oscar","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32"],"d":"Flair Software","u":"Flair Software, Titus","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscar_(video_game)"},{"t":"Out to Lunch","p":["Game Boy","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Mindscape","u":"Mindscape","g":"Platform","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Out_to_Lunch_(video_game)"},{"t":"Outback Joey","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outback_Joey"},{"t":"Outlander","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Mindscape","u":"Mindscape","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outlander_(video_game)"},{"t":"OutRun 2019","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Hertz","u":"Sega, SIMS Co., Ltd.","g":"Racing","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OutRun_2019"},{"t":"OutRunners","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega AM1","u":"Sega","g":"Racing","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OutRunners"},{"t":"Overdrive","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Psionic Systems","u":"Team17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overdrive_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"P.T.O. II","p":["PC-9800 series","MS-DOS"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Turn-based strategy, tactical wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P.T.O._II"},{"t":"Pac-Attack","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco, Namco Hometek, Nintendo, Namco/Philips Media","g":"Puzzle","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pac-Attack"},{"t":"Panel Action Bingo","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Locus","u":"FCI","g":"Action/Adventure","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panel_Action_Bingo"},{"t":"Panic!","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Sega, Office I","u":"Sega, Data East USA","g":"Puzzle, Point and click","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panic!_(video_game)"},{"t":"Party Quiz Mega Q","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Quiz game","r":"November 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Party_Quiz_Mega_Q"},{"t":"Pathways into Darkness","p":["macOS"],"d":"Bungie","u":"Bungie","g":"First-person shooter, adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathways_into_Darkness"},{"t":"Patriot","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Artech Digital Entertainment","u":"Three-Sixty Pacific","g":"Wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patriot_(video_game)"},{"t":"Pelé!","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Radical Entertainment","u":"Accolade","g":"Sports","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pel%C3%A9!"},{"t":"Pepper's Adventures in Time","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure, educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pepper's_Adventures_in_Time"},{"t":"Perihelion: The Prophecy","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Morbid Visions","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perihelion:_The_Prophecy"},{"t":"PGA Tour Golf II","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Polygames","u":"EA Sports, Time Warner Interactive","g":"Sports","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PGA_Tour_Golf_II"},{"t":"Phantasy Star IV","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phantasy_Star_IV"},{"t":"Phantasy Star IV: The End of the Millennium","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"u":"Sega","g":"Role-playing (RPG)","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/phantasy-star-iv-the-end-of-the-millennium","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2g92.jpg","s":"You are Chaz Ashely, a rookie Hunter straight out of training who is supported by Alys Brangwin – a crafty veteran whose good looks and prowess with the blade have earned her an awesome reputation. The two Hunters along with a cast of supporting characters will go beyond Motavia to unwittingly save their entire solar system."},{"t":"Pink Goes to Hollywood","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"HeadGames, Manley & Associates","u":"TecMagik","g":"Platform","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink_Goes_to_Hollywood"},{"t":"Pirates! Gold","p":["MS-DOS","Genesis / Mega Drive","Windows"],"d":"MPS Labs","u":"MicroProse","g":"Action-adventure, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pirates!_Gold","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4wp9.jpg","s":"As with the original, this is a blend of strategy, action and adventure. Pirates! Gold lets you play the role of a beginning buccaneer in the 17th century Spanish Main, in search of fame and fortune. Each town in this untamed raw region has different surprises and dangers.\n\nHow you acquire stature is up to you; you can make your living through honest trade and the search for hidden treasure, or you can be a little more daring and attack and plunder ships. As you can choose a specific skill to stand out in, the game can vary each time. Sword fighting is played out in side-view action sequences."},{"t":"Plok!","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"Nintendo, Tradewest, Activision Japan","g":"Platform","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plok!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2eta.jpg","s":"Plok is a side-scrolling platform game developed by Software Creations and released for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System in 1993. The game is a traditional platform game starring a character named Plok."},{"t":"Plumbers Don't Wear Ties","p":["Windows"],"d":"United Pixtures","u":"United Pixtures, Kirin Entertainment, Limited Run Games","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plumbers_Don't_Wear_Ties","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1p5l.jpg","s":"He's a plumber, she's a daddy's girl. Only you can get them together, or tear them apart! Plumbers have everything: Greed, sex, spirituality, white-knuckle chases, shameful propositions, a nun, humor, true love, jaded love, jealousy, taut action, comedy, a bad guy, a good guy, a hero, spine-tingling suspense, a hot babe, brazen bravado, a damsel in distress, and a Hollywood ending! Plays like a game... feels like a movie!"},{"t":"Police Quest: Open Season","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_Quest:_Open_Season"},{"t":"Pop'n TwinBee","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami, Palcom Software","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pop'n_TwinBee","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25vd.jpg","s":"A cute shoot 'em up from Konami."},{"t":"Power Factor","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Hand Made Software","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Action, platform","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Factor_(video_game)"},{"t":"Power Factory Featuring C+C Music Factory","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Digital Pictures","u":"Sony Imagesoft","g":"Music Video Editor","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Factory_Featuring_C+C_Music_Factory"},{"t":"Power Instinct","p":["Arcade","PlayStation","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega Saturn","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Atlus, Noise Factory","u":"Atlus","g":"Fighting game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Instinct","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co53gj.jpg","s":"The original Arcade version of Power Instinct.\nThis version only has the arcade mode and multiplayer while the later ports to console added practice, battle, time attack and life attack game modes.\n\nPower Instinct is a one-on-one 2D fighting game in the tradition of Street Fighter 2, in which you select your character from a group of 8 unique fighters and attempt to defeat your opponents in successive fashion using your arcade reflexes and your character's unique moves."},{"t":"Power Strike","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Strike"},{"t":"Power Strike II","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Compile","u":"Sega","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"October 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Strike_II_(Game_Gear_video_game)"},{"t":"Power Strike II","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Compile","u":"Sega","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Strike_II_(Master_System_video_game)"},{"t":"Prehistorik 2","p":["Amstrad CPC","MS-DOS"],"d":"Titus France","u":"Titus France","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prehistorik_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co262j.jpg","s":"Prehistorik 2 takes up the story of its predecessor, for another caveman-era platform game. The hero must use his club to crush animal enemies, many of which then release bonus items to collect. He no longer needs to feed his clan, so there is no required minimum food target, unlike the original game. Other bonus items are located around the levels, while yet more can be found by using your club creatively and adventurously. There are bosses and passwrods after every few levels."},{"t":"Premier Manager 2","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Realms of Fantasy","u":"Gremlin Interactive","g":"Sports simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Premier_Manager_2"},{"t":"Prince of Persia 2: The Shadow and the Flame","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Broderbund","u":"Broderbund, Interprog, Titus Software","g":"Cinematic platform","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_of_Persia_2:_The_Shadow_and_the_Flame","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2ek1.jpg","s":"Prince of Persia 2: The Shadow and the Flame is a platform game released as the sequel to 1989's Prince of Persia. Prince of Persia 2 was published for MS-DOS and ported to Macintosh, Super NES, and FM Towns – a much shorter list of platforms than the first game. It was also available as a bonus that unlocks in the Xbox NTSC version of Prince of Persia: The Sands of Time."},{"t":"Princess Maker 2","p":["PC-9800 series","MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Gainax","u":"Gainax, Microcabin","g":"Raising simulation","r":"June 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Maker_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2qzx.jpg","s":"In this fantasy game, the player takes role of a war hero who raises a girl to the age of 18. At the end of the game, the daughter goes into a line of work; what this work is, how much talent she has for it, her marital life, and her overall happiness all depend on the player's actions."},{"t":"Prize Fighter","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Digital Pictures","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prize_Fighter_(video_game)"},{"t":"Pro Moves Soccer","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"BGS Developments, ZAT Productions","u":"ASCII Entertainment","g":"Sports game","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pro_Moves_Soccer"},{"t":"Pro Sport Hockey","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Jaleco","g":"Sports","r":"March 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pro_Sport_Hockey"},{"t":"Protostar: War on the Frontier","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Tsunami Media","u":"Tsunami Media","g":"Role-playing game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protostar:_War_on_the_Frontier"},{"t":"Puggsy","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega CD","Amiga"],"d":"Traveller's Tales","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"November 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puggsy"},{"t":"Putt-Putt Goes to the Moon","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Humongous Entertainment","u":"Humongous Entertainment","g":"Adventure","r":"October 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putt-Putt_Goes_to_the_Moon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2st3.jpg","s":"The second Junior Adventure featuring Putt-Putt the talking car finds him accidentally getting sent to the moon, and so he must find his way back."},{"t":"Puzzle & Action: Tant-R","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puzzle_&_Action:_Tant-R"},{"t":"Quantum Gate","p":["Windows","macOS","PlayStation","Sega Saturn"],"d":"Hyperbole Studios","u":"Media Vision Technology","g":"Interactive movie","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_Gate_(video_game)"},{"t":"Quest for Glory: Shadows of Darkness","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure/role-playing","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quest_for_Glory:_Shadows_of_Darkness","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25ev.jpg","s":"Shadows of Darkness is the fourth Quest for Glory game. It is a journey into the land of Mordavia - a setting influenced by Eastern Europe, particularly its Slavic areal. The hero had just begun to enjoy his victory in Tarna, when a spell zapped him into parts unknown, without a weapon, items, or even a source of light. Shortly after finding his way out of the dark cave, the hero meets a mysterious woman named Katrina. He traverses the swamps in the dark forest and finally reaches the town. However, the town is also engulfed by a foreboding atmosphere, and the hero cannot allow himself to relax. He must find out what is going on, why the town inhabitants are so hesitant and reluctant to help him, and what horrors are lurking outside of its walls. Life has never been quite so dangerous for our hero. He'll discover his quest soon enough - that is, if he lives.\n\nLike the previous Quest For Glory titles, the game is a combination of puzzle-solving adventure and role-playing. The player character can be a fighter, a thief, a magician, or a paladin. Importing a character from a preceding installment of the series is also possible. Once again the hero improves his skills by repeatedly using them (like throwing stones at a tree to improve the throwing ability, which might be needed to solve a puzzle).\n\nCombat system in this installment has undergone a major change. Battles are viewed from a side-scrolling perspective and are action-oriented. The protagonist is able to move freely, jump, and execute attacks and defensive moves, similarly to fighting games. The CD version of the game adds voice-overs to conversations as well as narrator's voice to text descriptions."},{"t":"R-Type III: The Third Lightning","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tamtex, Raylight Studios","u":"Irem, Jaleco, Retro-bit, Destination Software","g":"Side-scrolling shooter","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R-Type_III:_The_Third_Lightning","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25vl.jpg","s":"The evil Bydo Empire returns in this entry of the R-Type series. So climb into the latest R-Type vessel, the R-90, and blast through six side-scrolling levels on your way to destroy the Bydo mothership.\n\nGameplay is still the classic R-Type way with huge levels and bosses. With your detachable laser unit, you can fire in front or behind your ship. R-Type III now features three different kinds of laser unit, selectable at the start of a game. Each offers different laser types depending on the power-ups collected."},{"t":"Racing Aces","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Hammond & Leyland","u":"Sega","g":"Flight simulator, Shoot 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racing_Aces"},{"t":"Raging Fighter","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Fighting","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raging_Fighter"},{"t":"Raiden II","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Seibu Kaihatsu, Kinesoft, NuFX","u":"Seibu Kaihatsu, Fabtek, Nihon System, GameBank","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raiden_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2fn2.jpg","s":"Excellent sequel to the classic original \"Raiden\". As in the original, players control a fighter jet/spaceship through various levels while shooting down other planes and bombing tanks on the ground."},{"t":"Railroad Tycoon Deluxe","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Microprose","u":"MicroProse","g":"Business simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railroad_Tycoon_Deluxe"},{"t":"Ranger X","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"GAU Entertainment","u":"Sega","g":"Run and gun","r":"May 28, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranger_X","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2zhs.jpg","s":"Engage in airborne combat with jump-jets and weapons of incredible power or link up with your cyber cycle for high speed ground attacks. Each step inches you closer to your goal and the fight of your life. You are Ranger X... A mysterious lone warrior who has risen from the ashes to return peace and justice to a failed society. Eight post-apocalyptic levels of high-intensity action! Monstrous mechanical bosses await your every move. Use jump-jets to attack by air or transform into the Super Cyber Cycle and roll to victory!"},{"t":"Reach for the Skies","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Rowan Software","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Combat flight simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reach_for_the_Skies_(video_game)"},{"t":"Reader Rabbit 3","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reader_Rabbit_3"},{"t":"Return of the Phantom","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"MPS Labs","u":"MicroProse","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Return_of_the_Phantom"},{"t":"Return to Zork","p":["macOS","MS-DOS"],"d":"Activision","u":"Infocom","g":"Graphic adventure","r":"September 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Return_to_Zork","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co21y5.jpg","s":"Return to Zork: A mesmerizing interactive adventure of danger, intrigue and cunning."},{"t":"Richard Scarry's Busytown","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Novotrade, Pearson Software","u":"Paramount Interactive, Sega of America, Simon & Schuster","g":"Edutainment","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Scarry's_Busytown"},{"t":"Riddick Bowe Boxing","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Gear"],"d":"Malibu Interactive, Micronet Co., Ltd.","u":"Extreme Entertainment Group, Micronet","g":"Boxing/Fighting","r":"November 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riddick_Bowe_Boxing"},{"t":"Ridge Racer","p":["Arcade","Mobile phone"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco, Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge_Racer_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co20gc.jpg","s":"Ridge Racer was initially released on the Namco System 22 arcade system board and was later ported to the PlayStation console. It is the first title in the long-running Ridge Racer series of games released for arcades and home consoles. The game's objective is to finish in first place in a series of races. Ridge Racer was among the first racing games to utilise polygon graphics to its full potential."},{"t":"Ringworld: Revenge of the Patriarch","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Tsunami Games","u":"Tsunami Games","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ringworld:_Revenge_of_the_Patriarch"},{"t":"Rise of the Phoenix","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Turn-based historic strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rise_of_the_Phoenix_(video_game)"},{"t":"RoboCop Versus The Terminator","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear","Sega Master System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Virgin Games, NMS Software, Interplay Team Rivet, Unexpected Development","u":"Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Run and gun","r":"December 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RoboCop_Versus_The_Terminator","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2r6d.jpg","s":"In the crumbling ruins of a world under siege, man-machine meets machine-man as legendary cyborgs clash to decide the fate of the Earth. As Robocop, you must travel across time into a bloody future ruled by the mechanical forces of Skynet. You'll face attack from every angle, every moment. An unwavering army of Terminators, robotic dogs, spiders, Endoskeletons and a relentless arsenal of automated weapons await you in this veritable hell on earth. To destroy this enemy, free its hostages and finally pierce the dark heart of a killer computer. It will take something more powerful than plasma rifles and laser guns: the human mind that still burns within you."},{"t":"Rock n' Roll Racing","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Windows"],"d":"Silicon & Synapse","u":"Interplay Productions, Namco, Blizzard Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"June 4, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_n'_Roll_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3o1q.jpg","s":"The game pits four racers against each other, with up to two of them being player-controlled and the rest being AI opponents. Each race consists of four laps around tracks viewed from an isometric perspective, which enables players to discern the presence of frequent sloping sections spread throughout the game's various tracks. In addition to navigating the turns, racers must also maneuver hills and dips without falling or jumping over the guard rail at the track's edge."},{"t":"Rocket Knight Adventures","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platform, scrolling shooter","r":"August 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocket_Knight_Adventures","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co53mg.jpg","s":"He's courageous! He's clever! He's one good lookin' opossum. It's Sparkster the Rocket Knight, the heroic jet pack jockey with warp speed, quick wits and pumped up personality. After all, who else do you know can get a grip with his tail? Rocket through 7 epic stages of animalistic adventure, home of the hugest, strangest enemy pig creatures imaginable. (In fact, your mission is crammed with more ham than a Hollywood premier.) Destroy the Emperor who's on a porcine power trip that will take him to the Key to the Pig Star. In every stage you'll be moving, flying and riding in a new direction to escape opossum punishment. You're the thrust-meister controlling Sparkster's jet pack and his assault sword. Confront mechanized menaces like the Giant Pigbot, the Drill Of A Lifetime, and the snappy Crab Rangoon. Things take a turn for the worst in the room of rotating gravity where Axle Gear, the Black Knights awaits you. And you've never seen anything like the unreal mirrored lava pools where things will reflect badly on you. The tricks, the traps and challenges never end. But the world as you know it will if you don't grind those pigs for sausage."},{"t":"Rocky Rodent","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Platform","r":"July 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocky_Rodent"},{"t":"Rollerblade Racer","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Designer Software, Radiance Software, Tahoe Software Productions","u":"Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Alternative sports","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollerblade_Racer"},{"t":"Rolling Thunder 3","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Now Production","u":"Namco Hometek","g":"Run and gun","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling_Thunder_3"},{"t":"Rolo to the Rescue","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Vectordean","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Platform","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolo_to_the_Rescue"},{"t":"Romancing SaGa 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Square","u":"Square, Square Enix","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romancing_SaGa_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co24oa.jpg","s":"The Emperor or Empress of Avalon begins the game as King Leon and later plays as his various heirs down through the game years. Leon hands his kingdom over to his son, Gellard. At the end of Gellard's reign, the player is able to choose his or her own successor and pass on Gellard's abilities to them. At the end of each generation, he or she will be given a choice of four heirs. Their identity is based on a combination of chance and anyone whom the player has assisted or allied within in a past generation."},{"t":"RPG Jinsei Game","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Takara","u":"Takara","g":"Adventure, Life simulation game, Board game","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RPG_Jinsei_Game"},{"t":"Run Saber","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hori Electric","u":"Atlus, Ludi Games, Imagineer","g":"Action, platform","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run_Saber"},{"t":"Rusty","p":["PC-9800 series","MS-DOS"],"d":"C-Lab","u":"C-Lab","g":"Platform","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rusty_(video_game)"},{"t":"Sailor Moon","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Angel","u":"Angel, Bandai","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"August 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sailor_Moon_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2hig.jpg","s":"Sailor Moon (or Bishoujo Senshi Sailor Moon) is a beat 'em up video game developed by Angel in 1993, and ported to the Super Nintendo Entertainment System. It was localized in Europe exclusively in French in 1994. The Mega Drive version was developed and published by Ma-Ba, although certain elements were recycled from the Super NES version."},{"t":"Sam & Max Hit the Road","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"LucasArts","u":"LucasArts, Disney Interactive","g":"Graphic adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sam_&_Max_Hit_the_Road","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co30yv.jpg","s":"Sam & Max Hit the Road is a graphic adventure video game released by LucasArts during the company's adventure games era. The game was originally released for MS-DOS in 1993 and for Mac OS in 1995. A 2002 re-release included compatibility with Windows. The game is based on the comic characters of Sam and Max, the \"Freelance Police\", an anthropomorphic dog and \"hyperkinetic rabbity thing\". The characters, created by Steve Purcell, originally debuted in a 1987 comic book series. Based on the 1989 Sam & Max comic On the Road, the duo take the case of a missing bigfoot from a nearby carnival, traveling to many Americana tourist sites to solve the mystery."},{"t":"Samurai Aces","p":["Arcade","Windows","Mobile phone"],"d":"Psikyo, APXSoft","u":"Banpresto, Taito, 505 Games, Noritong","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"April 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samurai_Aces"},{"t":"Samurai Shodown","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK, Takara, JVC Musical Industries, Tectoy","g":"Fighting","r":"July 7, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samurai_Shodown_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6a7y.jpg","s":"In early Japan, a man named Shiro Tokisada Amakusa preaches a heretic religion. Little does anyone know that Amakusa is really a servant of the evil Ambrosia, who took over Amakusa's body. Ambrosia wishes to shroud the world in darkness. Now, choose between 12 warriors (and 3 bonus characters) to fight for the honor of destroying Amakusa, and fulfilling a mission."},{"t":"Sango Fighter","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sango_Fighter"},{"t":"Saturday Night Slam Masters","p":["Arcade","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","FM Towns"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Fighting","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturday_Night_Slam_Masters","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9p01.jpg","s":"Slam Masters is an arcade conversion of Capcom's wrestling game of the same name.\n\nTen wrestlers with different fighting styles and individual special attacks are available. They are controlled with three buttons; one for jumping, one for attacking and one for pinning. When close to your opponents, you can grab them to perform various moves ranging from ordinary attacks to body slams to throws. Since the action takes place in a ring, it is also possible to climb the turnbuckle or use the ropes to your advantage. You can also leave the ring for a limited amount of time to fight with different weapons that can be found outside the ring.\n\nApart from a standard Single Match mode, the SNES version also features a Battle Royal mode for up to four players. This mode is not available in the Genesis version, which features a Death Match mode that can not be found in the SNES version. A Death Match is a no rules match in which weapons can be used used and the rope is replaced with barbed wire."},{"t":"ScubaVenture: The Search for Pirate's Treasure","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Softdisk","g":"Action","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ScubaVenture:_The_Search_for_Pirate's_Treasure"},{"t":"SEAL Team","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Electronic Arts","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Tactical shooter","r":"June 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEAL_Team_(video_game)"},{"t":"Second Samurai","p":["Amiga","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Vivid Image","u":"Psygnosis, Sony Electronic Publishing","g":"Action, Platform, Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Samurai"},{"t":"Secret of Mana","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Mobile phone","Windows"],"d":"Square","g":"Action role-playing","r":"August 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secret_of_Mana","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2u6w.jpg","s":"Initially released in Japan in 1993, Secret of Mana took the world by storm with its innovative real-time battle system and gorgeously rendered world. It continues to stand out among other action RPGs for its seamless gameplay that anyone from beginner to veteran can enjoy.\n\nOne of the most memorable elements of the Mana series is the Ring Command menu system. With the single press of a button, a ring-shaped menu appears on the screen, where players can use items, change weapons, and do a variety of other actions without needing to switch screens. This Ring Command menu system for which the Mana series is so well known was first introduced in Secret of Mana and has since appeared in most games in the series.\n\nPlay as Randi and his two companions, Primm and Popoi, as they adventure all around the world. At the center of our epic story is the mystical power of Mana. Battle the empire in its quest for control of Mana. Befriend the eight elementals who wield the forces of nature itself. Numerous encounters await at every turn."},{"t":"Secret of the Stars","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Role-playing","r":"November 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secret_of_the_Stars"},{"t":"Seek and Destroy","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","MS-DOS"],"d":"Vision Software","u":"Safari Software, Mindscape, THQ","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seek_and_Destroy_(1996_video_game)"},{"t":"Sega World Tournament Golf","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sega_World_Tournament_Golf"},{"t":"SegaSonic the Hedgehog","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega AM3","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SegaSonic_the_Hedgehog","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1zsw.jpg","s":"SegaSonic the Hedgehog is an isometric platforming game with three characters who shares identical controls and abilities. The characters are controlled with one action button which lets the player perform a spin jump, and a trackball which is used to move the characters around. Each trackball and corresponding action button are color-coded, meaning the blue ones are for Sonic, red ones are for Mighty and yellow ones are for Ray.\n\nThe premise of the game is to reach the end of an isometric course without dying. This objective is similar to other video games in the Sonic the Hedgehog series. Yet, it is also different in the sense that instead of a typical platforming game, the game is centered entirely around running as fast as possible on a linear course from a never-ending barrage of threats, including giant boulders, lava flows, rogue gears, falling stalactites, and other traps. When the player is hit by a dangerous obstacle or hazard, they lose health from the Health Gauge, which can be refilled by collecting Rings that found around the arena. Upon completing each level, the game tallies up how many Rings were grabbed. Extra points are also received if over half of the level's Rings were found.\n\nThe game only has a few enemies and one boss that appears midway through the game. At the end of the final level, the player is given twenty seconds to escape from the self-destructing tower. Failure results in an instant Game Over."},{"t":"Sengoku 2","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Beat 'em up, hack and slash","r":"February 18, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sengoku_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coaw8h.jpg","s":"In Sengoku 2, a warrior from the future is summoned by a prophetic princess to feudal Japan, where an evil monarch has gained supernatural powers to control the dead. The player must battle through armies of reanimated souls to restore peace to a land where the weak have fallen prey to the strong."},{"t":"Serf City: Life is Feudal","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Blue Byte Software","u":"Blue Byte Software, SSI (Strategic Simulations, Inc.)","g":"Real Time Strategy (RTS), Simulator, Strategy","r":"June 30, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/serf-city-life-is-feudal","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4v92.jpg","s":"A strategy game with cartoony graphics and an innocent line in humour. At the start of the level you choose a starting point, the intention being to get lots of flat land as well as resources to mine and ideally existing sources of trees, stones and water (for fish). Your people are vying for supremacy with up to 3 others.\n\nThe gameplay focuses on resource management. Each building requires a certain amount of wood (and stones for some of them) to be constructed and requires particular resources to perform its function successfully. Food must be produced (either fish, bread (requiring a windmill, grain-farmer and baker) or pork (requiring a pig-farmer and butcher as well as the grain-farm) to feed the people working in mines to produce the iron, coal and gold (as well as additional stones).\n\nHuts and Watch-Towers are built to expand your territory, sometimes at the expense of an enemy's land (clever play involves targeting an area where your opponent has a crucial building, thus compromising his production).\n\nTo finally win the level, you must defeat your opponents. Combat is fought one-at-a-time by the little soldiers and a victory results in all surrounding buildings being lost.\n\nThe game features 30 preset missions. 6 tutorials missions will help beginners to learn the game mechanics. The game also offers the possibility to play semi-randomly (based on a 16-number key) generated maps. The map size varies from small maps, for quick matches, to large maps to, depending on how much RAM is available, huge maps, for very long matches as the fact that the in-game statistics can be displayed on a 50-hour scale illustrates. These semi-random maps can be played in single-player mode but can also be played by 2 players on one system, if you have 2 mice, in which case the screen is vertically split."},{"t":"Shadow of the Comet","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series","Linux"],"d":"Infogrames","u":"Infogrames, I•Motion","g":"Adventure","r":"March 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_of_the_Comet"},{"t":"Shadow President","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"DC True","u":"DC True","g":"Geopolitical simulation","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_President"},{"t":"ShadowCaster","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Raven Software","u":"Origin Systems, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Role-playing","r":"October 28, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ShadowCaster"},{"t":"Shadowrun","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Data East, Laser Beam Entertainment","g":"Action role-playing","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadowrun_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2yws.jpg","s":"Shadowrun, based on the pen-and-paper RPG of the same name, is a futuristic sci-fi/fantasy game, taking inspiration from Neuromancer, Blade Runner, and other cyberpunk stories."},{"t":"Shanghai III","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Success, Kuusoukagaku","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Mahjong solitaire","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghai_III"},{"t":"Sherlock Holmes: Consulting Detective Vol. III","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"ICOM Simulations","g":"Adventure, interactive movie","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherlock_Holmes:_Consulting_Detective_Vol._III"},{"t":"Shining Force II","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sonic! Software Planning","u":"Sega","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"October 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shining_Force_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co97f0.jpg","s":"As a student of Astral the wizard, you and your friends study all the usual subjects: reading, writing, and sword fighting. As your time at school draws to an end, dark events are taking place on the island of Grans. The two Jewels which seal the Door to Darkness have been stolen, allowing a creature of awesome power to be unleashed. Finding where the Jewels were taken, and why, is a task only the bravest of adventurers can undertake."},{"t":"Shining Force: The Sword of Hajya","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Sonic! Software Planning","u":"Sega","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shining_Force:_The_Sword_of_Hajya"},{"t":"Shinobi III: Return of the Ninja Master","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Hack and slash, platform","r":"July 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinobi_III:_Return_of_the_Ninja_Master","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9788.jpg","s":"Shinobi III: Return of the Ninja Master, released as The Super Shinobi II in Japan, is the fourth game in the Shinobi series and a direct sequel to The Revenge of Shinobi."},{"t":"Shinseiki Odysselya","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Vic Tokai","u":"Vic Tokai","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"June 18, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinseiki_Odysselya"},{"t":"Sid & Al's Incredible Toons","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sid_&_Al's_Incredible_Toons"},{"t":"Silva Saga II: The Legend of Light and Darkness","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"SETA","u":"SETA","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silva_Saga_II:_The_Legend_of_Light_and_Darkness"},{"t":"Silverball","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Digital Extremes, Epic MegaGames","u":"MicroLeague, Team17","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silverball"},{"t":"SimFarm","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Maxis","u":"Maxis","g":"Construction and management simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SimFarm","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5vh8.jpg","s":"SimFarm was developed by Maxis as a spinoff of SimCity, and allowed the player to simulate the running of a farm. It was released in 1993 for PC Dos, and was released a year later for Macintosh. It has become a classic, and began the subsequent development of the farm simulation subgenre, although Maxis did not make any sequel to the game in the way they have for SimCity."},{"t":"Simon the Sorcerer","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","MS-DOS","Acorn Archimedes","Windows"],"d":"Adventure Soft","u":"Adventure Soft","g":"Point-and-click adventure","r":"September 27, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_the_Sorcerer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cob3gz.jpg","s":"Simon the Sorcerer is an adventure game that was released by Adventure Soft on 2 January 1993 for Amiga and DOS formats. The game's name comes from the account of Simon the Sorcerer in Acts 8. The game includes parodies of various popular books and fairy tales, including Rapunzel, The Lord of the Rings, The Chronicles of Narnia, Jack and the Beanstalk and the Three Billy Goats Gruff."},{"t":"Simulman","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Simulmondo","u":"Simulmondo","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simulman_(video_game)"},{"t":"Sink or Swim","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Atari ST","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear"],"d":"Odysseus Software","u":"Zeppelin Games","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sink_or_Swim_(video_game)"},{"t":"SkyRoads","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"BlueMoon Software","u":"Creative Dimensions","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SkyRoads_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co30zd.jpg","s":"In SkyRoads the goal is to get to the end of the level using the ship's jumping capability without crashing, falling into the infinite void, exploding, running out of oxygen, or running out of fuel."},{"t":"Slater & Charlie Go Camping","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Educational","r":"May 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slater_&_Charlie_Go_Camping"},{"t":"Sleepwalker","p":["Amiga","Amiga CD32","Commodore 64","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"CTA Developments","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Platform, Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleepwalker_(video_game)"},{"t":"Smash Tennis","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco, Virgin Interactive","g":"Sports","r":"June 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smash_Tennis","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3sjl.jpg","s":"Smash Tennis is a tennis game that was created by Namcot for the Super Nintendo in 1993. It was released in Europe as Smash Tennis by Virgin Interactive. A North American release of the game wouldn’t be seen until February 2020 where it would be part of the Nintendo Switch Online lineup.\n\nThe controls of Smash Tennis work similar to other tennis games and especially Namco's previous tennis game World Court Tennis: the player moves the athlete over the court and presses buttons with the correct timing in order to win the match. This time there is no story mode, but a tournament mode instead. Here the goal is to win the Grand Slam by playing various tournament matches. However. a match only has a single set. There are 20 athletes (both male and female) to choose from which have different advantages and drawbacks."},{"t":"Soccer Kid","p":["Amiga","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Krisalis Software","u":"Krisalis Software, Hot Point Software, Yanoman Corporation, Ocean Software","g":"Platform","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soccer_Kid","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1p6w.jpg","s":"Soccer Kid is a side-scrolling platform game released in 1993 for various home computers and consoles. Players control a young boy who uses his soccer ball to defeat enemies and overcome obstacles. The game’s story follows his quest to recover pieces of the World Cup trophy scattered across different countries. Levels feature themed environments based on locations such as England, Italy, Russia, Japan, and the United States."},{"t":"Socket","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Vic Tokai","u":"Vic Tokai","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Socket_(video_game)"},{"t":"Solar Winds","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Stone Interactive Media","u":"Epic MegaGames","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_Winds"},{"t":"Soldiers of Fortune","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"The Bitmap Brothers","u":"Renegade Software, Spectrum HoloByte, Inc., MicroProse Software, Inc., R-Comp Interactive, Amiga, Inc., WarnerActive, Glu Mobile, Mastertronic Group Ltd, Tec Toy, Crawfish Interactive","g":"Shooter, Adventure, Arcade","r":"February 1, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/soldiers-of-fortune","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3qco.jpg","s":"Soldiers of Fortune (Chaos Engine in the EU) is a run-and-gun overhead shooter. Sixteen levels (in four groups of four) await the players in the game. The basic gameplay is in an open air setting, with bridges across rivers and other features in the maps. The visuals are in a 'metallic and blue' style. There are six characters available in the game, each with different strengths and weaknesses, and different starting prices."},{"t":"Sonic CD","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"September 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_CD","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co53t2.jpg","s":"A fast-paced platformer that follows Sonic as he races through time-traveling zones to rescue past and future versions of stages and stop Dr. Robotnik’s time-altering plans. Combining classic Sonic speed and precision with CD-quality music, animated cutscenes, and branching time-based routes, it rewards quick reflexes and exploration to unlock true endings."},{"t":"Sonic Chaos","p":["Game Gear","Sega Master System"],"d":"Aspect","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"November 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_Chaos"},{"t":"Sonic Spinball","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega Technical Institute","u":"Sega","g":"Pinball","r":"November 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_Spinball"},{"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog Chaos","p":["Game Gear","Sega Master System"],"d":"Aspect","u":"Sega, Tec Toy","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"October 25, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/sonic-the-hedgehog-chaos","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3kly.jpg","s":"Dr. Robotnik has stolen the Red Chaos Emerald, causing the other five to \"lose balance\" and scatter themselves into a parallel universe. As a result of the instability, South Island is now sinking into the ocean. It is up to Sonic and Tails to find the Chaos Emeralds and restore harmony to the universe, or else the innocent residents of South Island will find themselves taking a long swim."},{"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog: Spinball","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega Technical Institute","u":"Tec Toy, Sega Enterprises, Ltd., Sega, Sega Corporation","g":"Platform, Puzzle, Pinball","r":"November 23, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/sonic-the-hedgehog-spinball","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2v8e.jpg","s":"Dr. Eggman (AKA Dr. Robotnik) is once again turning the animals of Mobius into robots using his monstrous contraption, the Veg-O-Fortress. Only Sonic can penetrate the Pinball Defense System to free the animals, retrieve the Chaos Emeralds, and put a stop to Dr. Eggman’s fiendish plans!"},{"t":"Sorcer Striker","p":["Arcade","FM Towns","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Raizing","u":"Able Corporation","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorcer_Striker"},{"t":"SOS","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment, Vic Tokai","g":"Survival, Adventure","r":"May 28, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SOS_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Soul & Sword","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Pandora Box","u":"Zamuse","g":"Role-playing game","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soul_&_Sword"},{"t":"Space Hulk","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Electronic Arts","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Real-time tactical first-person shooter","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Hulk_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3dho.jpg","s":"\"WE ARE THE VANGUARD OF HONOUR. THE CUTTING EDGE OF THE BLOOD ANGELS. WE BRING DEATH.\"\n\nThe legendary Space Hulk returns to Windows PC, Mac and Linux in this epic turn based strategy game. Set in the Warhammer 40,000 universe, an intriguing story of legendary battles and heroic actions unfolds. Play as a small squad of fearless Space Marine Terminators who attempt to reclaim their honor by mounting an assault on a derelict space hulk infested by hordes of vicious tyranid Genestealers.\n\nTake control of the Adeptus Astartes of the Blood Angel chapter and guide them towards glory, while battling fierce Xenos and uncovering ancient relics in the infested confines of the massive Space Hulk named “Sin of Damnation”.\n\nThe game is a 3D digital turn based strategy game that recreates the classic claustrophobic board game experience in both singleplayer and multiplayer.\n\nCustomers purchasing Space Hulk will receive a free copy of the Deathwing Terminator DLC for Warhammer 40,000: Storm of Vengeance by EutechnyxKey Features:The first Space Hulk computer game in 17 years! Available on multiple platforms.\nSet in the hugely popular Warhammer 40,000 universe! Official license of the legendary boardgame from Games Workshop.\nTurn-based tactical strategy game! Based on the popular and iconic board game mechanics.\nBlood Angel Terminators! Fight the ferocious Genestealers with heroes of the mighty Blood Angels.\nFearsome Genestealers with challenging AI! Meet unforgiving hordes of Genestealers!\nThematic 3D environment! Experience the dark labyrinth of a Space Hulk as never before..\nSingle player campaign based on the \"Sin of Damnation\" space hulk! Take on the Sin of Damnation campaign, which includes 3 brand new prequel missions and the 12 missions from the classic board game.\nMultiplayer head-to-head recreating the board game experience against a friend! Get in the hot seat and battle it out with a friend.\nCross platform multiplayer between Windows PC, Mac, Linux and iOS! Play asynchronous against friends and foes on multiple platforms.\nNow with online Co-op mode!"},{"t":"Space Invaders DX","p":["Arcade","PC Engine"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Fixed shooter","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Invaders_DX"},{"t":"Space Quest V","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","r":"March 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Quest_V"},{"t":"Space Quest V: The Next Mutation","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra","g":"Point-and-click, Puzzle, Adventure","r":"February 5, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/space-quest-v-the-next-mutation","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5v4u.jpg","s":"After travelling back and forth through time in the previous game, Roger Wilco is back in the Starfleet Acadamy, serving as both a cadet and a janitor. Cheating his way through the Starfleet Aptitude Test, Roger is finally given the rank of captain, his own ship (a garbage scow) and a mission: to explore strange new worlds (which no man in his right mind would explore), to seek out new life and new civilizations (which grew out of the massive amounts of trash Roger will collect on his way), to boldly go where no man has gone before. Step by step Roger will have to unveil a galaxy-wide biohazardous material dumping scheme, solve the mystery of the disappearance of a fellow Starfleet captain and his ship, and confront an agent of an old nemesis."},{"t":"Spelling Jungle","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Bright Star Technology","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Educational, Puzzle, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spelling_Jungle"},{"t":"Spinmaster","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Data East, Onan Games","u":"SNK, G-Mode","g":"Platform","r":"December 16, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinmaster","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coa34q.jpg","s":"Spinmaster is a platform/beat 'em up arcade game developed and released by Data East in 1993. The story follows the main character Johnny and his sidekick Tom as they battle their way through treasure-seeking, mad scientist, Dr. De Playne's henchmen in a quest to save Johnny's girlfriend Mary and get their hands on a hidden treasure.\n\nBoth playable characters are armed with yo-yos but can also upgrade to other weapons (such as throwing stars, icicle daggers and guided missiles) to defeat all enemies and help them open treasure chests."},{"t":"Splatterhouse 3","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Now Production","u":"Namco","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"March 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splatterhouse_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4sld.jpg","s":"Splatterhouse 3 is a video game released by Namco for the Mega Drive/Genesis in 1993. It is the sequel to Splatterhouse 2 and was one of the first games to be given a rating by Sega's own Videogame Rating Council."},{"t":"Sports Illustrated: Championship Football & Baseball","p":["Game Boy","Game Gear"],"d":"Malibu Interactive, Halestorm, Unexpected Development","u":"THQ","g":"Sports","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sports_Illustrated:_Championship_Football_&_Baseball"},{"t":"Star Fox","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo EAD, Argonaut Software","u":"Nintendo","g":"Rail shooter","r":"February 21, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Fox_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co99og.jpg","s":"Star Fox, released as Starwing in Europe, is a 1993 rail shooter video game developed and published by Nintendo, with assistance from Argonaut Software, for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System. The first game in the Star Fox series, Star Fox follows Fox McCloud and the rest of the Star Fox team defending their homeworld of Corneria against the attacking forces of Andross."},{"t":"Star Trek: Judgment Rites","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Interplay Productions, MacPlay","u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Adventure game","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Judgment_Rites","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2jp2.jpg","s":"Return to command of the USS Enterprise in this classic.\nTrue episodic format beams eight original point-and-click missions into your computer. Featuring the cast members and sound effects from Star Trek: The Original Series, Judgment Rites puts you on course for a galaxy of excitement!"},{"t":"Star Trek: The Next Generation","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Game Gear"],"d":"Imagineering Inc.","u":"Absolute Entertainment","g":"Large spaceship simulation","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_The_Next_Generation_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Star Wars Chess","p":["MS-DOS","Sega CD"],"d":"The Software Toolworks","u":"Mindscape","g":"Chess","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Wars_Chess"},{"t":"Star Wars: Rebel Assault","p":["MS-DOS","Sega CD","macOS","3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"LucasArts","u":"LucasArts","g":"Rail shooter, interactive movie","r":"November 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Wars:_Rebel_Assault","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1p63.jpg","s":"It is a period of civil war. Rebel starships, striking from a hidden base, have won their first victory against the evil Galactic Empire; which, under the command of Darth Vader, rules the galaxy with an iron fist. The Empire, however, is far from defeated, and many young pilots have joined the Rebels in the hopes of restoring freedom to the galaxy.\n\nRebel Assault was one of the first CD-ROM games and the first Star Wars game with the full original motion video. It's a side story of a young pilot in the rebel army. He came from Tatooine but he is just a regular pilot. The game takes you to some key battles, like taking down a star destroyer, raiding an Imperial base or destroying the Death Star"},{"t":"Star Wars: X-Wing","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Windows"],"d":"Totally Games","u":"LucasArts, U.S. Gold","g":"Space simulation","r":"March 4, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Wars:_X-Wing_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2rez.jpg","s":"Star Wars: X-Wing is the first LucasArts DOS computer game set in the Star Wars universe, as well as the lead title in the X-Wing combat flight simulator game series. The player's character flies starfighters, including the eponymous X-wing, for the Rebel Alliance as part of a narrative that precedes and parallels the events of Star Wars Episode IV: A New Hope."},{"t":"Stardust","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Bloodhouse","u":"Bloodhouse","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stardust_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Starlord","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Third Millennium Software","u":"MicroProse, Retroism, Night Dive Studios","g":"Strategy game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starlord_(video_game)"},{"t":"Stellar 7: Draxon's Revenge","p":["3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Dynamix","g":"First-person shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stellar_7:_Draxon's_Revenge"},{"t":"Strahl","p":["3DO Interactive Multiplayer","Sega Saturn"],"d":"Data East","u":"Pioneer Productions, Panasonic","g":"Action Interactive Movie","r":"December 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strahl_(video_game)"},{"t":"Street Fighter II: Special Champion Edition","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Sega, Tec Toy, Capcom","g":"Fighting","r":"September 27, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/street-fighter-ii-special-champion-edition-c6119b36-b741-4f18-b839-ef685f020f7f","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3t2i.jpg","s":"Street Fighter II: Special Champion Edition is an expanded version of Street Fighter II: Champion Edition with new features from Street Fighter II Turbo, released originally for the Sega Genesis/Mega Drive line of consoles. There are eight fighters to choose from, each with a unique fighting style and special attacks."},{"t":"Strike Commander","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series","Windows"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Combat flight simulation game","r":"April 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strike_Commander","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5wvi.jpg","s":"After the success of the Wing Commander games, Chris Roberts and Origin turned their eye to developing a story-focused combat flight simulation. The result was Strike Commander."},{"t":"Stronghold","p":["MS-DOS","FM Towns","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Stormfront Studios","u":"Strategic Simulations, Inc.","g":"City-building game, RTS","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stronghold_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Subtrade","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Century Interactive","u":"Boeder Software","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subtrade"},{"t":"Subwar 2050","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Particle Systems","u":"Interplay Entertainment","g":"Submarine simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subwar_2050"},{"t":"Super Air Diver","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Copya System","u":"Asmik Ace, Vic Tokai, Sunsoft","g":"Combat flight simulation","r":"July 16, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Air_Diver"},{"t":"Super Air Zonk: Rockabilly-Paradise","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Dual Corporation","u":"Hudson Soft, Turbo Technologies","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"July 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Air_Zonk:_Rockabilly-Paradise"},{"t":"Super Back to the Future II","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Daft","u":"Toshiba EMI","g":"Platformer","r":"July 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Back_to_the_Future_II"},{"t":"Super Battleship","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Synergistic Software","u":"Mindscape","g":"Strategy","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Battleship"},{"t":"Super Bomberman","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Produce!","u":"Hudson Soft, Sony Electronic Publishing, Living Mobile GmbH","g":"Action, maze, party","r":"April 28, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Bomberman","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co69t1.jpg","s":"Super Bomberman is the first video game in the Bomberman series to appear on the Super Nintendo Entertainment System. It is also the first four-player game to be released on the Super NES."},{"t":"Super Caesars Palace","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear"],"d":"The Illusions Gaming Company, Teeny Weeny Games","u":"Virgin Games, Coconuts Japan","g":"Casino","r":"August 8, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Caesars_Palace"},{"t":"Super Chinese World 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Action RPG","r":"October 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Chinese_World_2"},{"t":"Super Conflict","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Manley & Associates","u":"Vic Tokai","g":"Strategy","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Conflict"},{"t":"Super Dimension Fortress Macross II","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"Banpresto","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Dimension_Fortress_Macross_II"},{"t":"Super Dimension Fortress Macross: Scrambled Valkyrie","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Winkysoft","u":"Zamuse","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"October 29, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Dimension_Fortress_Macross:_Scrambled_Valkyrie"},{"t":"Super Fighter","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"C&E, Super Fighter Team","u":"C&E, Super Fighter Team","g":"Fighting","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Fighter"},{"t":"Super Formation Soccer II","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"June 11, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Formation_Soccer_II"},{"t":"Super Goal! 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Jaleco","g":"Sports","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Goal!_2"},{"t":"Super Godzilla","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Advance Communication Company","u":"Toho","g":"Action","r":"December 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Godzilla","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8r83.jpg","s":"Super Godzilla is a 16-bit Godzilla game where the player can take control of Toho's famous big green lizard as they rampage through Japan. The game is divided into two main gameplay modes, Movement and Battle. Movement has the player travel through the city using the D-pad and a map located on the bottom of the screen with the objective being to destroy certain objectives such as enemy humans and buildings as well as pick up power-ups. There is an animation in the screen above the map that shows the results of their movements and attacks.\n\nBattle mode kicks in when the player locates an enemy on the map, like Biollante or Mechagodzilla, and is taken to a side-scrolling cinematic-style battle where the player must use punches, tail whips and the famous fire breath to drain the energy of the opposing monster. However, the Fighting Spirit system is present which is in a sense a morality meter where the player must pick the right attacks at the right time in order to connect certain attacks. Choosing the wrong attacks or failing to connect with them will cause the meter to drop and make Godzilla more vulnerable to attack."},{"t":"Super Keiba","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Keiba"},{"t":"Super Mario All-Stars","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo EAD","u":"Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"July 14, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Mario_All-Stars"},{"t":"Super Mario Bros.","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo Entertainment Analysis & Development","u":"Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"July 14, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/super-mario-bros--3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5k7b.jpg","s":"A remaster of the original Super Mario Bros., released exclusively as part of the Super Mario All-Stars bundle."},{"t":"Super Mario Bros. 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo Entertainment Analysis & Development","u":"Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"July 14, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/super-mario-bros-2--2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7yq8.jpg","s":"A remaster of the original Super Mario Bros. 2 (the American port of Yume Koujou: Doki-doki Panic), released exclusively as part of the Super Mario All-Stars bundle."},{"t":"Super Mario Bros. 3","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo Entertainment Analysis & Development","u":"Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"July 14, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/super-mario-bros-3--2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7qpe.jpg","s":"A remaster of the original Super Mario Bros. 3, released exclusively as part of the Super Mario All-Stars bundle."},{"t":"Super Mario Bros.: The Lost Levels","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo Entertainment Analysis & Development","u":"Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"July 14, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/super-mario-bros-dot-the-lost-levels","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5k7g.jpg","s":"A remaster of the original Super Mario Bros. 2 (previously only released in Japan), released exclusively as part of the Super Mario All-Stars bundle."},{"t":"Super Methane Bros.","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Methane_Bros."},{"t":"Super Slam Dunk","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Park Place Productions","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Traditional basketball simulation","r":"July 16, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Slam_Dunk"},{"t":"Super Slap Shot","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Ringler Studios","u":"Virgin Games, Altron","g":"Traditional ice hockey simulation","r":"August 20, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Slap_Shot"},{"t":"Super Star Wars: The Empire Strikes Back","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sculptured Software, LucasArts","u":"JVC Musical Industries, Victor Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"November 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Star_Wars:_The_Empire_Strikes_Back","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25xq.jpg","s":"Pursued across the galaxy, you and your Rebel Forces now marshall new strength on the remote ice world of Hoth. Although tracked by probe droids, attacked by ice monsters, and confronted by an army of gigantic Imperial Walkers, you must not give in. You are the Alliance's only hope. Learn the ways of the Force, then test your skills in this fast-paced, action-packed sequel to the hit game Super Star Wars."},{"t":"Super Street Fighter II: The New Challengers","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Fighting, Arcade","r":"September 10, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/super-street-fighter-ii-the-new-challengers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6yje.jpg","s":"Super Street Fighter II: The New Challengers is a 2D fighting game that expands the original Street Fighter II roster with four new characters: Cammy, Dee Jay, T. Hawk, and Fei Long. Players choose from 16 fighters, each with unique moves, to compete in one-on-one matches. The game introduces updated graphics, rebalanced gameplay, and new special moves, continuing the World Warrior Tournament storyline."},{"t":"Super Strike Eagle","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"MicroProse","u":"Asmik Ace Entertainment, MicroProse","g":"Air combat simulation","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Strike_Eagle"},{"t":"Super Turrican","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Factor 5","u":"Seika Corp., Hudson Soft, Tonkin House, Factor 5","g":"Run and gun","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Turrican_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co97dq.jpg","s":"The lost colony of Alterra is a completely man-made lifeworld abandoned long ago in a nearby galaxy. Alterra is actually five colonies in one. Each self-contained habitat has been separately bio-engineered by a powerful ecosystem generation network known as a Multiple Organism Unit Link. MORGUL, for short. Early colonists used MORGUL to render Alterra inhabitable. But a cataclysmic quake severed all system interface functions, and MORGUL murderously rebelled. The few colonists lucky enough to escape told a grim tale of a higher intelligence gone berserk.\n\nFor generations, mankind sought a return to Alterra. Finally, genetic science created a saviour: Turrican, a mutant warrior, bio-engineered for the task of planetary reclamation. In the meantime, MORGUL has diligently twisted Alterran life forms to his brutal, destructive purposes. Thus, Turrican's challenges consist in eliminating hostile organisms from Alterra's five multi-level worlds and, finally, destroying the three faces of MORGUL."},{"t":"Super World Court","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Sports","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_World_Court"},{"t":"Superfrog","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Team17","u":"Team17","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superfrog","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1p8k.jpg","s":"Superfrog is a 2D platformer developed and published by Team17, originally released for the Amiga in 1993. Players control a super-powered frog who must traverse a variety of levels, overcome obstacles, and defeat enemies using different power-ups. The game is noted for its challenging platforming sections and a humorous, whimsical design, and it has since been ported to multiple platforms."},{"t":"Superior Soldiers","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Fighting","r":"December 9, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superior_Soldiers"},{"t":"Survival Arts","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Scarab","u":"Sammy","g":"Fighting","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Survival_Arts"},{"t":"Světák Bob","p":[],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sv%C4%9Bt%C3%A1k_Bob"},{"t":"Sylphia","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Compile","u":"Tonkin House","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"October 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylphia_(video_game)"},{"t":"Syndicate","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Amiga CD32","macOS","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","FM Towns","PC-9800 series","Atari Jaguar","3DO Interactive Multiplayer","Sega CD","Acorn Archimedes"],"d":"Bullfrog Productions","u":"Electronic Arts, Mindscape, Ocean Software, R-Comp","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"July 2, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syndicate_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5385.jpg","s":"Syndicate is a strategic action game set in a dystopian future where corporate entities vie for global dominance. Players control a syndicate, expanding their influence across a world map divided into territories. Starting from a European base, players deploy agents to infiltrate and conquer adjacent regions through covert operations and violence. Once a territory is captured, players can impose taxes to increase revenue, but must balance this with the risk of inciting rebellions. The game involves managing resources, dispatching agents to eliminate threats both foreign and domestic, and defending against rival syndicates' incursions. Success requires careful strategic planning, efficient resource management, and the ability to outmaneuver opponents on a global scale. The ultimate goal is to achieve world domination by controlling all territories and overcoming all challenges."},{"t":"Syndicate: American Revolt","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Bullfrog Productions","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Real-time tactics","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syndicate:_American_Revolt"},{"t":"Tajemnica Statuetki","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Metropolis Software House","u":"Metropolis Software House","g":"Adventure","r":"February 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tajemnica_Statuetki"},{"t":"Take a Break! Pinball","p":["Windows"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Pinball","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Take_a_Break!_Pinball"},{"t":"TaskMaker","p":["macOS"],"d":"Storm Impact","u":"XOR Corporation, Storm Impact","g":"Role-playing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TaskMaker"},{"t":"Technoclash","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Zono, BlueSky Software","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Action","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technoclash"},{"t":"Tecmo Cup Football Game","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Tecmo, Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Sports game simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tecmo_Cup_Football_Game"},{"t":"Tecmo World Cup '93","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"SIMS","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tecmo_World_Cup_'93"},{"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles III: Radical Rescue","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Metroidvania","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles_III:_Radical_Rescue","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2doz.jpg","s":"The whole sewer crew, except Michelangelo, is captured in a most heinous hideout. Who could be behind this travesty? Who else. Shredder has returned as Cyber Shredder - half-man, half-machine and he's created this twisty-turny fun house of Turtle torture. Use Turtle strategy and all new moves to help Michelangelo rescue the gang from Cyber Shredder's Fortress."},{"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles: Tournament Fighters","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Fighting game","r":"December 3, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles:_Tournament_Fighters","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2dgq.jpg","s":"This game's controls use a four-button scheme (two punches and two kicks, weak and strong). A particular feature is the possibility to use a super special attack. In order to achieve this, the player must fill a green bar under the life bar, by hitting their opponents. Once full, the player must press the two strong attack buttons simultaneously. There is also the option of enhancing the speed of the game, making the fights more intense but also more difficult to follow.\n\nIn addition to the main and versus modes, there is a story mode in which the Turtles must rescue April O'Neil and Splinter from Karai's clutches. The Turtles must travel across the US in their Turtle Blimp, defeating other fighters and collecting information. Only the four of them can be playable whereas the other characters (as well as a turtle clone) are the opponents. There is no Mutagen Meter in story mode. There is also a watch mode, which features computer-controlled characters."},{"t":"Tengai Makyō: Fuun Kabukiden","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Red Company, Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tengai_Maky%C5%8D:_Fuun_Kabukiden"},{"t":"Terminator 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Bits Studios","u":"Flying Edge, LJN","g":"Action","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminator_2_(16-bit_video_game)"},{"t":"Terminator 2: Judgment Day","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Bits Studios","u":"LJN, Playtronic","g":"Shooter, Hack and slash/Beat 'em up, Adventure","r":"September 1, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/terminator-2-judgment-day","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coby8h.jpg"},{"t":"Tetris 2","p":["Game Boy","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Nintendo, Bullet-Proof Software","g":"Puzzle","r":"September 21, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetris_2_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3a71.jpg","s":"SNES release of \"Tetris 2\".\n\nThe mesmerizing fun of Tetris returns - and the challenge escalates to new heights! Test your dexterity, tease your brain and rack up points with Tetris 2. Your split-second decisions lead you to a new dimension in puzzle-solving action! Play alone or challenge a friend in simultaneous split-screen action. A tougher Tetris with more shapes, more components, 30 levels - and unlimited solutions! If you loved the international game sensation Tetris, you'll be wild for the newest dimension in puzzle fun: Tetris 2!"},{"t":"Tetris Battle Gaiden","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Bullet-Proof Software","u":"Bullet-Proof Software","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 24, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetris_Battle_Gaiden"},{"t":"TFX","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Digital Image Design, Tomcat System","u":"Ocean Software, Imagineer","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TFX_(video_game)"},{"t":"The 7th Guest","p":["MS-DOS","CD-i"],"d":"Trilobyte","u":"Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Interactive movie, puzzle adventure","r":"April 28, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_7th_Guest","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2sep.jpg","s":"The 7th Guest is a horror adventure described as \"the first Interactive Drama\", with plentiful pre-rendered 3D graphics, live action video clips, and an original, orchestrated musical score. The atmosphere is dense, the puzzles difficult and the experience as a whole is completely horrifying."},{"t":"The 7th Saga","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Produce!","u":"Enix","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_7th_Saga","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8kzr.jpg","s":"The game takes place on a world called Ticondera. 5000 years ago, a divine being named Saro defeated an evil entity named Gorsia with the power of seven runes. In the years since then, the runes have been scattered across the globe. Lemele, the son of Saro, was born 100 years ago, and became a hero when he defeated the demon Gariso. Now 100 years old, Lemele has become the benevolent and powerful ruler of the world.\n\nThe 7th Saga is the localized version of the Super Famicom game Elnard. It is infamous for its high difficulty, whereas its Japanese counterpart is much easier. The 7th Saga also features some other changes, such as different character sprites."},{"t":"The Adventures of Buratino","p":[],"d":"Copper Feet","u":"Compact-Studio","g":"adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Buratino_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Adventures of Ninja Nanny & Sherrloch Sheltie","p":["Windows"],"d":"Silicon Alley","g":"Interactive fiction, Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Ninja_Nanny_&_Sherrloch_Sheltie"},{"t":"The Amazon Trail","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Windows"],"d":"MECC","u":"MECC","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Amazon_Trail"},{"t":"The Backyard","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Presage Software","u":"Broderbund","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Backyard_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Beverly Hillbillies","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Synergistic Software","u":"Capstone Software","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Beverly_Hillbillies_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Blues Brothers: Jukebox Adventure","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Titus France","u":"Titus France","g":"Platform","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blues_Brothers:_Jukebox_Adventure"},{"t":"The Chaos Engine","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Amiga CD32","MS-DOS","Acorn Archimedes","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Mobile phone","Windows","macOS","Linux"],"d":"The Bitmap Brothers","u":"Renegade Software","g":"Run and gun","r":"March 16, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Chaos_Engine"},{"t":"The Dungeon","p":["Acorn Archimedes"],"d":"Martin Dennett, John Parker, David Postlethwaite, Gordon J. Key","u":"The Fourth Dimension","g":"First-person, Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Dungeon_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"The Excellent Dizzy Collection","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Interactive Studios","u":"Codemasters","g":"Action, puzzle","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Excellent_Dizzy_Collection"},{"t":"The Fidgetts","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Game Over Production","u":"Elite Systems, GX Media Co, Jaleco","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fidgetts"},{"t":"The Flash","p":["Sega Master System","Game Boy"],"d":"Probe Software, Equilibrium","u":"Sega, THQ","g":"Action","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Flash_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Flintstones","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Taito, Santos","u":"Taito, Sega","g":"Platform","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Flintstones_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4eb8.jpg","s":"This is a platform game based on the famous Flintstones series. The game features many original Flintstones characters, such as Fred, Wilma, Barney, and others. Instead of the usual \"save the princess\" or \"liberate the world\" kinds of a plot, \"The Flintstones\" is mainly based on small quests: Fred has to retrieve a lost item, etc. The large levels are divided into smaller stages. In the end of each level there is a boss battle. The gameplay involves mostly jumping and hitting the enemies on the heads with a club. The health is measured by hearts. During the game Fred will find many useful items, including some that will increase his health level."},{"t":"The Flintstones: The Surprise at Dinosaur Peak","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Taito, Sol Corporation","u":"Taito","g":"Platform","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Flintstones:_The_Surprise_at_Dinosaur_Peak"},{"t":"The Gorilla Man","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gorilla_Man_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Grandest Fleet","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Fogstone Enterprises","u":"Quantum Quality Productions","g":"Turn-based tactics","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Grandest_Fleet"},{"t":"The Incredible Machine","p":["macOS","MS-DOS","Windows","FM Towns","PC-9800 series","3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"Jeff Tunnell Productions","u":"Sierra On-Line, Dynamix","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Incredible_Machine_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"The Journey Home: Quest for the Throne","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Wolf Team","u":"Telenet Japan","g":"Action role-playing","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Journey_Home:_Quest_for_the_Throne"},{"t":"The Journeyman Project","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Presto Studios","u":"Presto Studios, Sanctuary Woods, Bandai","g":"Adventure","r":"January 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Journeyman_Project_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Jungle Book","p":["Sega Master System","Game Gear","MS-DOS"],"d":"Syrox Developments, Eurocom, East Point Software","u":"Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Jungle_Book_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Labyrinth of Time","p":["Amiga CDTV","Amiga CD32","MS-DOS","Amiga","macOS"],"d":"Terra Nova Development, The Wyrmkeep Entertainment Co.","u":"Electronic Arts, The Wyrmkeep Entertainment Co.","g":"Graphic adventure","r":"June 1, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Labyrinth_of_Time"},{"t":"The Last Eichhof","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Eichhof"},{"t":"The Lawnmower Man","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS","Game Boy"],"d":"The Sales Curve, Atod","u":"THQ, Storm, Coconuts Japan, Time Warner Interactive","g":"Interactive movie, platform, shooter","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lawnmower_Man_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2dp8.jpg","s":"The Lawnmower Man is the game based on the 1992 movie of the same name (itself loosely based on a Stephen King short story) starring pre-Bond fame Pierce Brosnan as Dr. Lawrence Angelo, a scientist working for Virtual Space Industries in \"Project 5\", a secret research that attempts to increase the intelligence of primates using psychotropic drugs and VR training.\n\nThe twists of the game are true-3D level connectors, based on the CG sequences of the movie, usually all involving avoiding obstacles (and the occasional shooting) at high speed in the VR world. There are four different (Virtual World, Cyber War, Cyber Run and Cyber Tube), each taking a slightly different approach (Virtual World is seen in first person, the objective being dodging obstacles such as trees and archs and reach the exit, Cyber War similar to Virtual World but with some shooting stops, Cyber Run is seen in third person and requires occasional shooting of obstacles, while Cyber Tube is a fast travel with plenty of enemies in a VR tunnel)."},{"t":"The Legend of Bishin","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Magifact","u":"Magifact","g":"Beat 'em up, action, racing","r":"December 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Bishin"},{"t":"The Legend of Kyrandia 2: The Hand of Fate","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Westwood Studios","u":"StarCraft, Virgin Interactive Entertainment, Sold Out Software, Electronic Arts","g":"Point-and-click, Puzzle, Adventure","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/the-legend-of-kyrandia-2-the-hand-of-fate","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1y7s.jpg","s":"The land of Kyrandia is disappearing piece by piece and you are the offbeat, young mystic who must voyage to the center of the world to break the curse and save it. Your surreal journey will twist even the sharpest of minds. Traveling through a vast weirdly beautiful terrain, you must concoct potions, cast spells and lure the help of some very bizarre inhabitants to uncover the secrets that doom Kyrandia.\n\nCrafty Kyrandian veterans from the first Fables & Fiends adventure, The Legend of Kyrandia, may recognize their favorite land of giant frogs, talking trees and really strange berries. But this isn't just the same old Kyrandia! Challenging puzzles, new characters, and lots of self-conscious wisecracks from our heroine make Hand of Fate more challenging than it's predecessor."},{"t":"The Legend of Kyrandia: Hand of Fate","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Westwood Studios","u":"Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Kyrandia:_Hand_of_Fate"},{"t":"The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Nintendo EAD","u":"Nintendo","g":"Action-adventure","r":"June 6, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Link's_Awakening","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3nnt.jpg","s":"The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening is the first title in the series to be released on a handheld system and the only Zelda title on the original Game Boy. The gameplay is mostly similar to its predecessor with a top-down perspective, however there are short sections in dungeons where the game switches to a side-scrolling view with platforming elements. For this new adventure, Link must go onto each of the 7 temples to retrieve a musical instrument that will help awaken the Wind Fish. Along the way, he must search the land and uncover hidden treasures and items that will allow him to progress on his journey. Since its release, Link's Awakening has been popular among fans and critics."},{"t":"The Life Stage: Virtual House","p":["3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"Microcabin","u":"Matsushita Electric Corporation of America","g":"Construction simulation, Adventure","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Life_Stage:_Virtual_House"},{"t":"The Lost Vikings","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Silicon & Synapse","u":"Interplay Productions, Blizzard Entertainment","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lost_Vikings","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co65yo.jpg","s":"The Lost Vikings is a puzzle-platform video game developed by Silicon & Synapse (now Blizzard Entertainment) and published by Interplay.\n\nThe game features infinite opportunities of retries in case the player loses one of the Vikings.\n\nIn 2015, The Lost Vikings were added as a playable hero in Heroes of the Storm, allowing players to control the three Vikings as individual units, making a unique departure from the typical heroes available.\n\nCharacter abilities:\n\nAll three Vikings (Erik, Baleog and Olaf) have three health points which they can lose by getting hurt by enemies or by falling from great heights, and the ability to carry and use items, mainly keys, bombs, and food (which restore health points). Each of the Vikings has a unique set of abilities:\n\nErik can run faster than the other two, can jump, and can bash through some walls (and enemies) with his helmet.\nBaleog can kill enemies with his sword, or from a distance with his bow (and a \"life-time supply of arrows\"). The bow can also be used to hit switches from a distance.\nOlaf can block enemies and their projectiles with his shield, and use his shield as a hang glider. Olaf's shield can also be used as a stepping stone for Erik to enable him to reach higher ground, which would not be possible without the shield."},{"t":"The Peace Keepers","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"December 17, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Peace_Keepers"},{"t":"The Punisher","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Punisher_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobubo.jpg","s":"The Punisher is a 1993 beat 'em up arcade game. It stars the Marvel Comics' anti-hero the Punisher and co-stars S.H.I.E.L.D. agent Nick Fury as the second player's character as they embark on a mission to kill the crime lord the Kingpin and bring down his organization. Whilst following the same general formula as Capcom's previous beat 'em ups, the game has a range of usable weapons and a comics-style presentation."},{"t":"The Real Ghostbusters","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Kemco","u":"Kemco, Activision","g":"Action, platform","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Real_Ghostbusters_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"The Ren & Stimpy Show Presents: Stimpy's Invention","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"BlueSky Software","u":"Sega, Tec Toy","g":"Adventure","r":"December 31, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/the-ren-and-stimpy-show-presents-stimpys-invention","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2rnk.jpg","s":"In one player the player controls either Ren or Stimpy and the computer controls the other character who will simply follow; however, the player can swap the character they control at almost any time during game play. In two player, players controls one character each, both within the same screen (no split screen). In one or two player several special moves can be performed by both characters standing close and a player pressing certain buttons simultaneously, the move differs depending on which buttons are pressed and whether the player is controlling Ren or Stimpy. The ability of swapping characters during game play in one player means the player can perform all the special moves not just those available for one character. The moves include: Stimpy throwing Ren to cover long distance such as over a hole."},{"t":"The Settlers","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Blue Byte","u":"Blue Byte, SSI","g":"City-building, real-time strategy","r":"June 30, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Settlers_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"The Terminator: Rampage","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Bethesda Softworks","u":"Bethesda Softworks","g":"First-person shooter","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Terminator:_Rampage"},{"t":"The Third World War","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Micronet","u":"Micronet, Extreme","g":"Simulation, Strategy","r":"November 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Third_World_War_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Treehouse","p":["MS-DOS","Apple II","macOS","FM Towns","Windows"],"d":"Broderbund","u":"Broderbund","g":"Educational","r":"September 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Treehouse_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Twisted Tales of Spike McFang","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Red Company","u":"Naxat Soft, Bullet-Proof Software","g":"Action RPG","r":"March 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Twisted_Tales_of_Spike_McFang"},{"t":"The Ultimate Haunted House","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Byron Preiss Multimedia Brooklyn Multimedia","u":"Microsoft Home","g":"Adventure game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ultimate_Haunted_House"},{"t":"The Wizard of Oz","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Manley & Associates","u":"SETA Corporation","g":"Platformer","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wizard_of_Oz_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Thoroughbred Breeder","p":[],"d":"Hect","u":"Hect","g":"Alternative sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thoroughbred_Breeder"},{"t":"Thunder Dragon 2","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"NMK, Sammy","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder_Dragon_2"},{"t":"Tiles of the Dragon","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Softdisk","g":"Mahjong solitaire","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiles_of_the_Dragon"},{"t":"Time Slip","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"The Sales Curve","u":"Vic Tokai","g":"Action, Platformer","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Slip"},{"t":"Tinhead","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"MicroProse","u":"Ballistic, Piko Interactive","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinhead"},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures 2: Montana's Movie Madness","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platformer","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures_2:_Montana's_Movie_Madness"},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures: Buster's Hidden Treasure","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platform","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures:_Buster's_Hidden_Treasure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co96e1.jpg","s":"Hit it big when you join Buster Bunny on a 33 stage hunt for treasure. So loaded with hare-raising animation, it's like playing in a whacked-out Tiny Toon cartoon! Do you dare to set paw on this mysterious island? Trip through 7 tangly territories that include an overly-enchanted forest, caverns of bubbling lava-lava, secret underground seas, plains that are just plain crazy, a freaky factory, a mega mountain and a spooky shipwreck rumored to be dripping in 14 carrot gold! Save Babs Bunny and the rest of your pals along the way and you'll really see some kooky island hopping. With Gogo Dodo as your guide, you can be sure this adventure is packed with tricks, traps, and hidden bonus areas!"},{"t":"ToeJam & Earl in Panic on Funkotron","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Johnson Voorsanger Productions","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"December 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ToeJam_&_Earl_in_Panic_on_Funkotron","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2rpc.jpg","s":"ToeJam & Earl in Panic on Funkotron is a platform video game, developed by Johnson Voorsanger Productions and published by Sega originally in 1993.\n\nThe game concerns two alien protagonists, ToeJam and Earl, both of whom have escaped from Earth, where they had crash landed. After returning to their home planet of Funkotron, the duo discover a number of antagonistic Earthlings have stowed away on the spacecraft and are wreaking havoc across the planet. The player must hunt down these Earthlings and imprison them in jars before sending them back to Earth."},{"t":"Tom and Jerry","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_and_Jerry_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Tom Mason's Dinosaurs for Hire","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"SEGA Interactive Development Division","u":"Sega of America, Tec Toy","g":"Shooter","r":"July 2, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/tom-masons-dinosaurs-for-hire","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2zdw.jpg","s":"Unknown monsters and weird robotic creatures attack the Earth. For some reason, the only ones who can stop the invasion are the three Dinosaurs for Hire: Archie, a tyrannosaurus rex and their leader; the triceratops Lorenzo, lover of fine food, fine wine, and fine clothes, and the one-eyed, unpredictable stegosaurus Reese. Each dino has its own strengths and weaknesses.\n\nYou guide the dinosaur of your choice through the platform levels of the game. You are equipped with a gun: tons of enemies will attack you from all the sides, so your best bet will be walking with the fire button pressed down. You can shoot to all the directions and also engage in melee combat when the enemy is too close. Each level has a boss enemy waiting for you in the end. There is also a cooperative two-player mode."},{"t":"Tony Meola's Sidekicks Soccer","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Electro Brain, Pack-In-Video","g":"Sports","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Meola's_Sidekicks_Soccer"},{"t":"Top Gear 2","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Gremlin Interactive","u":"Kemco, Vic Tokai, Gremlin Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Gear_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co63nw.jpg","s":"This third-person racing game resembles Gremlin's earlier Lotus series, in terms of its general look and feel, right down to the text font used in the game. Your task is to race through 16 countries, each of which features four races, with many real-world circuits recreated in incongruous locations (such as the Monza layout in Ayers Rock, and the old Hockenheim in Vancouver).\n\nThese four-race blocks each represent their own mini-championship, in which you race against 19 cars, needing to finish in the top 10 to continue, and with the top 6 scoring points 10-6-4-3-2-1, and the respective amount of money in thousands. This can be spent on various upgrades, ranging from engine to tires (wet and dry) and from shocks (front, side and rear) to gearboxes. The ideal approach is to buy the more expensive versions ASAP, as you get no saving when upgrading. If you win the four-race championship, you get the next password.\n\nYou get a set amount of nitro boost to use during each race, although bonus nitros, money and instant speed-up token appear on many tracks. Hazards such as puddles, barriers and ramps are also frequent."},{"t":"Top Gun: Guts and Glory","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Konami","g":"Modern jet flight simulation","r":"January 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Gun:_Guts_and_Glory"},{"t":"Top Rank Tennis","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Pax Softnica","u":"Nintendo","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Rank_Tennis"},{"t":"Tornado","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Digital Integration","u":"Spectrum HoloByte","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tornado_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Torneko's Great Adventure","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Windows"],"d":"Chunsoft","u":"Chunsoft","g":"Role-playing, roguelike","r":"September 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torneko's_Great_Adventure"},{"t":"Towers: Lord Baniff's Deceit","p":["Atari ST"],"d":"JV Enterprises","u":"JV Enterprises, Vatical Entertainment, Telegames","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Towers:_Lord_Baniff's_Deceit"},{"t":"Toys","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Imagineering","u":"Absolute Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"April 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toys_(video_game)"},{"t":"Transarctica","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Silmarils","u":"Silmarils","g":"Action-adventure, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transarctica"},{"t":"Traps 'n' Treasures","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Roman Werner","u":"Starbyte Software, Krisalis Software","g":"Platform, action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traps_'n'_Treasures"},{"t":"Trevor McFur in the Crescent Galaxy","p":["Atari Jaguar"],"d":"Atari Corporation","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Horizontally scrolling shooter","r":"November 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trevor_McFur_in_the_Crescent_Galaxy"},{"t":"True Golf Classics: Wicked 18","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"T&E Soft","u":"Bullet-Proof Software, T&E Soft, Sega","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/True_Golf_Classics:_Wicked_18"},{"t":"Tuff E Nuff","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Fighting game","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuff_E_Nuff","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4vlj.jpg","s":"Following a great war that destroys most of the earth, Tuff E Nuff sees four of the earth's toughest people come together to do battle, and earn the right to attempt to depose the mighty ruler. Gameplay is standard beat 'em up style action, featuring most of the conventions of the genre. The characters have a range of kicks, punches and throws, and each fight at their own base. The 90-second time limit can be turned on or off. Fights are best-of-three, with points awarded for each hit, and for overall victory."},{"t":"Tuneland","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"7th Level","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuneland"},{"t":"Twisted: The Game Show","p":["3DO Interactive Multiplayer"],"d":"Studio 3DO","u":"Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Party","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twisted:_The_Game_Show"},{"t":"Ultima Underworld II: Labyrinth of Worlds","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"LookingGlass Technologies","u":"Origin Systems, Electronic Arts","g":"Action role-playing","r":"January 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultima_Underworld_II:_Labyrinth_of_Worlds","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2cq2.jpg","s":"In an attempt to conquer the world, The Guardian encases Castle Britannia and its occupants--including the Avatar--in a giant blackrock gem.\n\nThe game takes place one year after the events of Ultima 7: The Black Gate. To celebrate the defeat of the Guardian, Lord British invites the Avatar and his companions to his castle. However, in the night of the feast the Guardian attacks again, encasing the castle in a giant gem of blackrock. Deep in the sewers of the castle, another blackrock gem resides, being a teleporter to other worlds controlled by the Guardian. Now you, the Avatar, must travel to these worlds in order to free the castle from the Guardian's spell. This sequel to Ultima Underworld plays exactly as the original, except for a larger game world, a richer storyline and better graphics and sound."},{"t":"Ultima VII Part Two: Serpent Isle","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Origin Systems","g":"Role-playing game","r":"March 25, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultima_VII_Part_Two:_Serpent_Isle","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2zt4.jpg","s":"This game takes place 18 months after The Black Gate. Batlin, the leader of the Fellowship has disappeared, as well as Iolo's wife Gwenno. A map is found that points to the Serpent Isle where the Guardian is still planning to destroy Britannia. Lord British sends you, the Avatar, and your companions to this new land. Once there, you discover that a great imbalance is upon the isle and it is up to you to fix it. To do so, you must learn the philosophies of the original inhabitants of this land who worshipped giant serpents symbolizing the principles of Order, Chaos and Balance."},{"t":"Ultima: Runes of Virtue II","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Pony Canyon, FCI, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Role-playing","r":"November 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultima:_Runes_of_Virtue_II"},{"t":"Ultimate Domain","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Microïds","u":"Mindscape, The Software Toolworks","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimate_Domain"},{"t":"Ultimate Soccer","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear","Sega Master System"],"d":"Rage Software","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimate_Soccer"},{"t":"Ultrabots","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"NovaLogic","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"First-person shooter, Vehicle simulation","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultrabots"},{"t":"Undercover Cops: Hakaishin Garumaa","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Traditional game","r":"December 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undercover_Cops:_Hakaishin_Garumaa"},{"t":"Undersea Adventure","p":["Windows","MS-DOS"],"d":"Knowledge Adventure","u":"Knowledge Adventure, IBM","g":"Educational software","r":"December 13, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undersea_Adventure"},{"t":"Unnatural Selection","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Maxis","u":"Maxis","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unnatural_Selection_(video_game)"},{"t":"Uridium 2","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Andrew Braybrook","u":"Renegade Software","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uridium_2"},{"t":"Ushio to Tora: Shin'en no Daiyō","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Pixel, Tom Create","u":"Yutaka","g":"Role-playing game","r":"July 9, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ushio_to_Tora:_Shin'en_no_Daiy%C5%8D"},{"t":"Uzu Keobukseon","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Samsung","u":"Samsung","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"May 5, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uzu_Keobukseon"},{"t":"V for Victory: Gold-Juno-Sword","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Atomic Games","u":"Three-Sixty Pacific","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_for_Victory:_Gold-Juno-Sword"},{"t":"V for Victory: Market-Garden","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Atomic Games","u":"Three-Sixty Pacific","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_for_Victory:_Market-Garden"},{"t":"Variable Geo","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variable_Geo"},{"t":"Vay","p":["Sega CD","Steam"],"d":"Hertz","u":"SIMS, Working Designs, SoMoGa, Inc.","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"October 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vay_(video_game)"},{"t":"Vegas Stakes","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"Nintendo, Imagineer","g":"Casino","r":"April 10, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vegas_Stakes"},{"t":"Veil of Darkness","p":["MS-DOS","FM Towns","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Event Horizon Software","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Adventure, role-playing","r":"March 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veil_of_Darkness"},{"t":"Violent Storm","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violent_Storm"},{"t":"Virtua Fighter","p":["Arcade","R-Zone","Windows"],"d":"Sega AM2","u":"Sega","g":"Fighting","r":"October 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtua_Fighter_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co84c6.jpg","s":"A classic fighting game by Sega, considered one of the first 3D fighting games."},{"t":"Virtual Pinball","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Bill Budge","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Pinball simulation, Game creation system","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_Pinball"},{"t":"Voyeur","p":["CD-i","MS-DOS"],"d":"Philips POV Entertainment","u":"Philips Interactive, Interplay, MacPlay","g":"Interactive movie","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyeur_(video_game)"},{"t":"Waka Taka Ōzumō: Brothers Dream Match","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tomcat System","u":"Imagineer","g":"Sports sumo","r":"November 12, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waka_Taka_%C5%8Czum%C5%8D:_Brothers_Dream_Match"},{"t":"Walker","p":["Amiga"],"d":"DMA Design","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walker_(video_game)"},{"t":"Walls of Rome","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Mindcraft","u":"Action Sixteen","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walls_of_Rome_(video_game)"},{"t":"War in the Gulf","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Oxford Digital Enterprises","u":"Empire Interactive","g":"Simulation, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_in_the_Gulf"},{"t":"War of Aero","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Allumer","u":"Allumer","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_Aero"},{"t":"Warlords II","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Studies Group","u":"Strategic Studies Group","g":"Computer wargame, 4x","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warlords_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/s6z3cadeo1jwcbxyajw6.jpg","s":"Warlords is a strategic war game, fought out by eight different players, for the domination of the Kingdom of Illuria. It can be played by one to eight people, with the computer taking the remaining positions. The Kingdom of Illuria, after some severe internal disputes, has seethed under a sullen and uneasy peace. This truce has now disappeared, along with the arch-mage who imposed it. The eight empires of Illuria realize that this is their chance for total domination, a very unstable situation. Forces are being mobilized for conquest, and only one Supreme Warlord will prevail.\n\nYour objectives in Warlords are brutally simple: to eliminate all organized opposition to your rule. To do this, you will have to control all eighty cities in the lands of Illuria, and thereby eliminate all seven opponents. There are many ways to achieve this, and brute force is just one of them. you might ally with dragons or wizards, or have your Heroes search out and wield magical swords that will tip the battle in your favor. Clever military tactics and even economic warfare are possibilities too.\nWarlords II brought more continents to the game along with many new features and better graphics."},{"t":"Wayne's World","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Boy","MS-DOS"],"d":"Gray Matter, Radical Entertainment, Robert Fiorini & Associates","u":"THQ, Capstone","g":"Platform","r":"February 18, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayne's_World_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5p26.jpg","s":"\"Wayne's World, Wayne's World! Party time! Excellent! Woo, woo, woo!\"\n\nIt's Friday, it's 10:30 and like usual, Wayne and Garth are presenting their TV show. First they start off by reading a top-10 list of the worst games they recently played at the arcade. Then Wayne accounts on how he and Garth were suddenly sucked into a video game, and Garth was kidnapped by the mysterious Zantar. \"Weeuuu, weeuuu, weeuuu!\" FLASHBACK, and now you're suddenly in control of Wayne as he must embark upon a quest to save his good pal Garth.\n\nNot to be confused with the DOS license, this Wayne's World is a platform- style game. You must jump, fight and rock your way through 4 different levels which include Kramer's Music Store, Stan Mikita's Donut Store, Gas Works and Surburbia. Using Wayne's guitar as your sonic weapon, it's most excellent noise will bring death to various enemies. Will you save Garth from the evil purple clutches of Zantar? SCHWING!"},{"t":"Whale's Voyage","p":[],"d":"Neo Software","u":"Flair Software","g":"Role-playing","r":"February 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale's_Voyage"},{"t":"When Two Worlds War","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Impressions Games","u":"Impressions Games","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/When_Two_Worlds_War"},{"t":"Where in Space Is Carmen Sandiego?","p":["macOS","MS-DOS"],"d":"Broderbund","u":"Broderbund","g":"Educational","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Where_in_Space_Is_Carmen_Sandiego%3F"},{"t":"Who Killed Sam Rupert?","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Creative Multimedia Corporation","g":"Adventure, murder mystery","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Who_Killed_Sam_Rupert%3F"},{"t":"Wily & Right no RockBoard: That's Paradise","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Business simulation","r":"January 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wily_&_Right_no_RockBoard:_That's_Paradise"},{"t":"Wing Commander Academy","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Origin Systems","g":"Space combat simulation","r":"August 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing_Commander_Academy_(video_game)"},{"t":"Wing Commander: Privateer","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Space trading and combat simulator","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing_Commander:_Privateer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2khs.jpg","s":"About: 2669, Gemini Sector, Troy System. The Terran frontier between the Kilrathi Empire and the unknown. Privateer takes you to the seamy side of the Wing Commander universe. In the far reaches of space, you live by no man's rules but your own. The fringes are populated by a volatile mix of pirates, miners, mercs\ncutthroats, and Kilrathi, all struggling to make a life for themselves.\n\nIn this open-ended game you can choose from many paths. You start with a rusted scout ship and a handful of credits in your pocket It isn't much, but the universe is there for you to explore in search for profitable opportunities. Become a patient, law-abiding merchant, a smuggler far away from the normal space lanes, a pirate preying on the weak, or a professional mercenary hunting down criminal scum. Or just do whatever suits you at the time. At the end of the day, it's all about money and survival. Mostly money.\n\nAre you determined enough to forge your own destiny?"},{"t":"Winter Olympics","p":["Amiga","Genesis / Mega Drive","MS-DOS","Sega Master System"],"d":"Abstract Images, Unexpected Development, Tiertex","u":"U.S. Gold","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winter_Olympics_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Wiz 'n' Liz","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Amiga"],"d":"Raising Hell Software, Lunatic Software","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiz_'n'_Liz","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co87ck.jpg","s":"Wiz and Liz are magicians. They live on the planet Pum and brew potions and cast spells. They also tend to rabbit-like creatures called \"wabbits\". One day, a concoction goes awry and sends all the wabbits to distant parts of Pum. Wiz and Liz set off to look for them.\n\nThe game is an action platformer divided into several stages. In each one of them, either Wiz or Liz must collect wabbits before time runs out. The player must pick up floating letters that appear when a wabbit is collected, and use them to spell out a magic word shown at the top of the screen. Once the word has been spelled, the newly found wabbits leave fruit, stars, and clocks that increase the time limit. The time limit can also be extended by picking up a glowing orange orb which appears during the last ten seconds of the counter, adding thirty seconds to it."},{"t":"Words Worth","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Words_Worth"},{"t":"World Heroes 2","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES","PC Engine"],"d":"ADK, Agenda, Saurus","u":"SNK, Hudson Soft, Saurus, Takara","g":"Fighting","r":"April 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Heroes_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobu6g.jpg","s":"The time traveling tournament fighters are back! The original eight characters from the popular arcade hit return, along with six new characters hand-picked from several different eras and locations all through time. But a new boss awaits the victor... who or what is behind this new tournament? You'll have to get to him to find out!\n\nThis sequel features all-new stages and music, and improved animation and graphics. You can now reflect projectile moves at your opponent, and reverse normal throws if your timing is right! The original game's Death Match mode is also back, but with a new feature - the life bar will increase or decrease as you inflict or receive damage. Get your side's life bar to 100% and your enemy will lose."},{"t":"World Rally","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Zigurat Software","u":"Gaelco, Sigma, Atari Games","g":"Racing","r":"July 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Rally_(video_game)"},{"t":"World Rugby","p":["ZX Spectrum","Commodore 64","Atari ST","Amiga"],"d":"Brendan O'Brien, David Taylor","u":"Zeppelin Games","g":"Sports Management","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Rugby_(video_game)"},{"t":"World Series Baseball","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"ITL, BlueSky Software","u":"Sega of America","g":"Sports","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Series_Baseball_(1994_video_game)"},{"t":"Worlds of Legend: Son of the Empire","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Mindscape","u":"Mindscape","g":"Role-playing","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worlds_of_Legend:_Son_of_the_Empire"},{"t":"Writer Rabbit","p":["Apple II","MS-DOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Writer_Rabbit"},{"t":"WWF King of the Ring","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Gray Matter, Eastridge Technology","u":"LJN","g":"Fighting, sports","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_King_of_the_Ring_(video_game)"},{"t":"WWF Rage in the Cage","p":["Sega CD"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Professional wrestling, fighting, sports","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_Rage_in_the_Cage"},{"t":"WWF Royal Rumble","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Sports-based fighting","r":"June 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_Royal_Rumble_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2dir.jpg","s":"Control one of twelve World Wrestling Federation stars in a \"no holds barred brawl\" -- in other words, do whatever moves you want, from choke holds, piledrivers, and even chair slams, until your opponent is knocked out. You can even go outside the ring if you like. Several modes of play are available, including one-on-one, tag team (two-on-two), and three-on-three tag team. You can play as the following WWF wresters: Bret Hart, Razor Ramon, Randy Savage, Shawn Michaels, The Undertaker, Tatanka, Mr. Perfect, Crush, Ric Flair, Yokozuna, Ted Dibiase, and Lex Luger."},{"t":"X-Men","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Western Technologies Inc","u":"Sega","g":"Action, platform","r":"March 15, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Men_(1993_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4tus.jpg","s":"X-Men is a side-scrolling action game based on the comic book series of the same name. The player can control one of four playable characters: Gambit, Nightcrawler, Wolverine, or Cyclops. In addition, it is possible to call upon four other X-Men in when necessary."},{"t":"Xak III: The Eternal Recurrence","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns","PC Engine","PC Engine CD"],"d":"Microcabin","u":"Microcabin, NEC","g":"Action role-playing game","r":"April 23, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xak_III:_The_Eternal_Recurrence"},{"t":"Xplora1: Peter Gabriel's Secret World","p":["macOS","CD-i","Windows"],"d":"Real World Media","u":"MacPlay","g":"Music Game","r":"December 21, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xplora1:_Peter_Gabriel's_Secret_World"},{"t":"Yadamon","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yadamon"},{"t":"Yearn2Learn","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yearn2Learn"},{"t":"Yoshi's Safari","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo R&D1","u":"Nintendo","g":"Light gun shooter","r":"July 14, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoshi's_Safari","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6pz6.jpg","s":"Yoshi's Safari is the first and so far the only dedicated rail shooting game in the Mario franchise, and one of the few games to use the SNES's Super Scope light gun peripheral. Gameplay consists of shooting enemies before they attack. There are bosses along the way that must be destroyed before players can move on to the next area."},{"t":"Young Merlin","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Westwood Studios","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Action-Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Young_Merlin","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2vsq.jpg","s":"After trying to rescue a drowning young woman, the the hero of Young Merlin is drawn into a fantasy world which is threatened by the evil Shadow King. Naturally, it is your job to save this world and also to rescue the aforementioned woman who has been kidnapped by the Shadow King. This is done in typical action adventure fashion: you have to fight enemies using spells that are gathered during the progress of the game as well as solve puzzles and use several objects. What sets Young Merlin apart from other action adventures is the fact that there is no text in the entire game, instead, icons are used to represent conversations, furthermore, the game uses passwords,instead of a battery to record the players progress."},{"t":"Ys IV: Mask of the Sun","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tonkin House, Arc System Works","u":"Tonkin House, Taito","g":"Action role-playing","r":"November 19, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ys_IV:_Mask_of_the_Sun"},{"t":"Ys IV: The Dawn of Ys","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Action role-playing","r":"December 22, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ys_IV:_The_Dawn_of_Ys"},{"t":"Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon","p":["CD-i"],"d":"Animation Magic","u":"Philips Interactive Media","g":"Puzzle, Adventure","r":"October 10, 1993","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/zelda-the-wand-of-gamelon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co60tx.jpg","s":"Zelda: The Wand of Gamelon was the product of a compromise between Nintendo and Philips following their failure to release a CD-ROM based add-on to the Super Nintendo Entertainment System. It was developed in tandem with Link: The Faces of Evil and marks the first game where the eponymous princess Zelda is playable. The Wand of Gamelon differ from most conventional Zelda games as it is a platformer with a side-scrolling view, similar to The Adventure of Link. Stages are accessed from a world map, with more becoming available as Zelda clears an area or defeats a boss. The game have been subject to much criticism and Nintendo does not recognize it as part of the series."},{"t":"Zen: Intergalactic Ninja","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen:_Intergalactic_Ninja_(video_game)"},{"t":"Zero Team","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Seibu Kaihatsu","u":"Seibu Kaihatsu, Fabtek","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"May 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero_Team"},{"t":"Zero4 Champ","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Media Rings","u":"Media Rings","g":"Racing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero4_Champ"},{"t":"Zombies Ate My Neighbors","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"LucasArts","u":"Konami","g":"Run and gun","r":"September 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zombies_Ate_My_Neighbors","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2rxx.jpg","s":"In the game you are a male or female protagonist (Zeke or Julia), fighting the vampires, mummies, zombies, etc. on over 50 levels (not including secrets) and saving all of the people you find. There are defined number of neighbors you save on every level. When they are free then \"Exit\" door opens and you jump on next level.The levels differ from mall and grocery store to beaches and football fields. Using different weapons such as bazooka or water gun you have to kill the bosses such as huge babies, dragons or ants at the end of levels. You can play the game with two players cooperatively."},{"t":"Zone 66","p":["Windows"],"d":"Renaissance","u":"Epic MegaGames","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zone_66"},{"t":"Zool 2","p":["Amiga"],"d":"The Warp Factory, Gremlin Graphics, Imagitec Design","u":"Gremlin Graphics, Atari Corporation, Mumin Corporation, Amiga, Inc.","g":"Platform","r":"November 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zool_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8t9t.jpg","s":"The intergalactic gremlin ninja Zool returns, and this time his enemies in the game were named Krool and his assistant Mental Block, whose goal is to stifle the world's imagination, causing rampant boredom. In his fight Zool is aided by his female companion, named Zooz (in a red costume), and his faithful dog Zoon. The ending contained a hint at a possible further sequel"},{"t":"Zurk's Learning Safari","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Soleil Software, Presage Software","u":"Soleil Software","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zurk's_Learning_Safari"},{"t":"Étoile Princesse","p":["Sharp X68000"],"d":"Exact","u":"Exact","g":"Action role-playing, platform, puzzle, run and gun","r":"March 26, 1993","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89toile_Princesse"}];
